(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("databases",{"databases/relational-fundamentals-and-sql":{"html":"\u003cp>Almost every service you will run keeps its important data in a database. You do not need to design schemas all day, but you must read a query, understand what a table holds and know which commands are safe on a live system.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Tables, rows, primary keys and foreign keys\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>SELECT, JOIN, GROUP BY and filtering\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Safe habits for UPDATE and DELETE\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Operator queries: sizes, counts and running sessions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-relational-model\">The relational model\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>table\u003c/strong> holds rows with the same columns. A \u003cstrong>primary key\u003c/strong> identifies each row. A \u003cstrong>foreign key\u003c/strong> points at a row in another table and lets the database enforce that the link is valid.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"CREATE TABLE customers (\n  id    bigserial PRIMARY KEY,\n  email text NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n  name  text NOT NULL\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE orders (\n  id          bigserial PRIMARY KEY,\n  customer_id bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES customers (id),\n  total_cents integer NOT NULL CHECK (total_cents &gt;= 0),\n  created_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE TABLE\u003c/span> customers (\n  id    bigserial \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">PRIMARY KEY\u003c/span>,\n  email text \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">UNIQUE\u003c/span>,\n  name  text \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL\u003c/span>\n);\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE TABLE\u003c/span> orders (\n  id          bigserial \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">PRIMARY KEY\u003c/span>,\n  customer_id \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">bigint\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">REFERENCES\u003c/span> customers (id),\n  total_cents \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">integer\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CHECK\u003c/span> (total_cents \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>),\n  created_at  timestamptz \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DEFAULT\u003c/span> now()\n);\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Constraints (\u003ccode>NOT NULL\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>UNIQUE\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>CHECK\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>REFERENCES\u003c/code>) put the rules in the database, where every client has to obey them, not in each application.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-data\">Reading data\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT c.name, count(*) AS orders, sum(o.total_cents) / 100.0 AS revenue\nFROM customers c\nJOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id\nWHERE o.created_at &gt;= now() - interval '30 days'\nGROUP BY c.name\nHAVING count(*) &gt; 5\nORDER BY revenue DESC\nLIMIT 10;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> c.name, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">count\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">*\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> orders, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(o.total_cents) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">/\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">100.0\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> revenue\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> customers c\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN\u003c/span> orders o \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON\u003c/span> o.customer_id \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> c.id\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> o.created_at \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;=\u003c/span> now() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">interval\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;30 days&#x27;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> c.name\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">HAVING\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">count\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">*\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> revenue \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC\u003c/span>\nLIMIT \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>WHERE\u003c/code> filters rows before grouping, \u003ccode>HAVING\u003c/code> filters groups after\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>JOIN\u003c/code> combines rows from two tables on a condition\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>LIMIT\u003c/code> protects you from pulling a million rows into your terminal\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"changing-data-safely\">Changing data safely\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"BEGIN;\nSELECT count(*) FROM orders WHERE created_at &lt; '2020-01-01';\nDELETE FROM orders WHERE created_at &lt; '2020-01-01';\n-- check the row count reported matches your expectation, then:\nCOMMIT;   -- or ROLLBACK;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BEGIN\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">count\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">*\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> orders \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> created_at \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">&lt;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2020-01-01&#x27;\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DELETE\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> orders \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> created_at \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">&lt;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2020-01-01&#x27;\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">-- check the row count reported matches your expectation, then:\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COMMIT\u003c/span>;   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">-- or ROLLBACK;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Habits that prevent disasters:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Run the matching \u003ccode>SELECT\u003c/code> first and compare the count\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use a transaction so you can \u003ccode>ROLLBACK\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never run \u003ccode>UPDATE\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>DELETE\u003c/code> without a \u003ccode>WHERE\u003c/code> clause. Many clients can be set to refuse this.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Do large changes in small batches so locks and logs stay small\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Take a backup or snapshot before a risky change\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"operator-queries-postgresql\">Operator queries (PostgreSQL)\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_database_size(current_database()));\n\nSELECT relname, pg_size_pretty(pg_total_relation_size(relid)) AS size\nFROM pg_catalog.pg_statio_user_tables\nORDER BY pg_total_relation_size(relid) DESC\nLIMIT 10;\n\nSELECT pid, state, now() - query_start AS running_for, left(query, 60) AS query\nFROM pg_stat_activity\nWHERE state &lt;&gt; 'idle'\nORDER BY running_for DESC;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> pg_size_pretty(pg_database_size(current_database()));\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> relname, pg_size_pretty(pg_total_relation_size(relid)) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> size\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_catalog.pg_statio_user_tables\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> pg_total_relation_size(relid) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC\u003c/span>\nLIMIT \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>;\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> pid, state, now() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">-\u003c/span> query_start \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> running_for, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">left\u003c/span>(query, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">60\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> query\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_activity\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> state \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">&lt;&gt;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;idle&#x27;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> running_for \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>These answer three everyday questions: how big is it, what is taking space, and what is running now. Other engines have equivalents, such as \u003ccode>information_schema\u003c/code> tables and \u003ccode>SHOW PROCESSLIST\u003c/code> in MySQL.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"using-the-command-line\">Using the command line\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql &quot;host=db1.example.com dbname=shop user=readonly&quot; -c &quot;SELECT count(*) FROM orders;&quot;\npsql -h db1.example.com -U readonly shop -f report.sql\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">psql \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;host=db1.example.com dbname=shop user=readonly&quot;\u003c/span> -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT count(*) FROM orders;&quot;\u003c/span>\npsql -h db1.example.com -U \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">readonly\u003c/span> shop -f report.sql\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use a read-only account for investigation. Inside \u003ccode>psql\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>\\dt\u003c/code> lists tables, \u003ccode>\\d orders\u003c/code> describes one, and \u003ccode>\\x\u003c/code> switches to a readable vertical layout.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You run DELETE FROM orders; without a WHERE clause inside a transaction and notice immediately. What do you do?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Run ROLLBACK before committing. Nothing is permanent until COMMIT, so the rows return. If you had run it without a transaction in autocommit mode, only a backup or point-in-time recovery would bring them back.\u003c/p>\u003cp>That is why risky statements go inside BEGIN.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"app&gt;parse;parse&gt;store;store&gt;wal\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Where a query's data comes from\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application or psql\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"parse\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Parse and plan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"store\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Tables and indexes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"wal\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Write-ahead log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Application or psql to Parse and plan; 2, Parse and plan to Tables and indexes; 3, Tables and indexes to Write-ahead log.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application or psql \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sends SQL over a network connection.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An over-privileged account can change or delete anything.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"parse\">\u003cdt>Parse and plan \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The database checks syntax and chooses how to run it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A bad plan makes a simple query slow.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"store\">\u003cdt>Tables and indexes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rows are read from memory or disk.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cache misses turn queries into disk reads.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"wal\">\u003cdt>Write-ahead log \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Changes are logged before they are acknowledged.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A full log disk stops all writes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-delete-without-a-where\">Incident: the delete without a WHERE\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An engineer ran a cleanup query in a production console. The WHERE clause was cut off when pasting, and every row in a customer table was deleted.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql shop -c &quot;DELETE FROM customers&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql shop -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DELETE FROM customers&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">DELETE 1840223\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team restored from a point-in-time backup, losing twelve minutes of writes to reconcile by hand. Afterwards they required read-only consoles by default, a break-glass role for writes, transactions with a row-count check, and reviews for production data fixes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Investigating with a read-write account\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running unbounded queries on a busy primary\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that DDL such as \u003ccode>DROP TABLE\u003c/code> may not roll back in every engine\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Selecting \u003ccode>*\u003c/code> on wide tables during an incident\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-relational-model","text":"The relational model"},{"id":"reading-data","text":"Reading data"},{"id":"changing-data-safely","text":"Changing data safely"},{"id":"operator-queries-postgresql","text":"Operator queries (PostgreSQL)"},{"id":"using-the-command-line","text":"Using the command line"},{"id":"incident-the-delete-without-a-where","text":"Incident: the delete without a WHERE"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL tutorial","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"},{"t":"Constraints","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-constraints.html"},{"t":"Monitoring database activity","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/monitoring-stats.html"}],"text":"Almost every service you will run keeps its important data in a database. You do not need to design schemas all day, but you must read a query, understand what a table holds and know which commands are safe on a live system. What you will learn Tables, rows, primary keys and foreign keys SELECT, JOIN, GROUP BY and filtering Safe habits for UPDATE and DELETE Operator queries: sizes, counts and running sessions The relational model A table holds rows with the same columns. A primary key identifies each row. A foreign key points at a row in another table and lets the database enforce that the link is valid. CREATE TABLE customers ( id bigserial PRIMARY KEY , email text NOT NULL UNIQUE , name text NOT NULL ); CREATE TABLE orders ( id bigserial PRIMARY KEY , customer_id bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES customers (id), total_cents integer NOT NULL CHECK (total_cents >= 0 ), created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now() ); Constraints ( NOT NULL , UNIQUE , CHECK , REFERENCES ) put the rules in the database, where every client has to obey them, not in each application. Reading data SELECT c.name, count ( * ) AS orders, sum (o.total_cents) / 100.0 AS revenue FROM customers c JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id WHERE o.created_at >= now() - interval &#x27;30 days&#x27; GROUP BY c.name HAVING count ( * ) > 5 ORDER BY revenue DESC LIMIT 10 ; WHERE filters rows before grouping, HAVING filters groups after JOIN combines rows from two tables on a condition LIMIT protects you from pulling a million rows into your terminal Changing data safely BEGIN ; SELECT count ( * ) FROM orders WHERE created_at \u003c &#x27;2020-01-01&#x27; ; DELETE FROM orders WHERE created_at \u003c &#x27;2020-01-01&#x27; ; -- check the row count reported matches your expectation, then: COMMIT ; -- or ROLLBACK; Habits that prevent disasters: Run the matching SELECT first and compare the count Use a transaction so you can ROLLBACK Never run UPDATE or DELETE without a WHERE clause. Many clients can be set to refuse this. Do large changes in small batches so locks and logs stay small Take a backup or snapshot before a risky change Operator queries (PostgreSQL) SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_database_size(current_database())); SELECT relname, pg_size_pretty(pg_total_relation_size(relid)) AS size FROM pg_catalog.pg_statio_user_tables ORDER BY pg_total_relation_size(relid) DESC LIMIT 10 ; SELECT pid, state, now() - query_start AS running_for, left (query, 60 ) AS query FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state \u003c> &#x27;idle&#x27; ORDER BY running_for DESC ; These answer three everyday questions: how big is it, what is taking space, and what is running now. Other engines have equivalents, such as information_schema tables and SHOW PROCESSLIST in MySQL. Using the command line psql \"host=db1.example.com dbname=shop user=readonly\" -c \"SELECT count(*) FROM orders;\" psql -h db1.example.com -U readonly shop -f report.sql Use a read-only account for investigation. Inside psql , \\dt lists tables, \\d orders describes one, and \\x switches to a readable vertical layout. Predict first. You run DELETE FROM orders; without a WHERE clause inside a transaction and notice immediately. What do you do? Your guess Answer: Run ROLLBACK before committing. Nothing is permanent until COMMIT, so the rows return. If you had run it without a transaction in autocommit mode, only a backup or point-in-time recovery would bring them back. That is why risky statements go inside BEGIN. Where a query's data comes from Flow in order: 1, Application or psql to Parse and plan; 2, Parse and plan to Tables and indexes; 3, Tables and indexes to Write-ahead log. Service Step Data store Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Application or psql Service What it does: Sends SQL over a network connection. If it fails or is weak: An over-privileged account can change or delete anything. Parse and plan Step What it does: The database checks syntax and chooses how "},"databases/indexes-and-query-plans":{"html":"\u003cp>A table with ten rows is fast however you query it. A table with fifty million rows is only fast when the database can jump straight to the rows it needs. Indexes make that possible, and query plans show whether the database is using them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What an index is and when it helps\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The cost of indexes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading \u003ccode>EXPLAIN\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>EXPLAIN ANALYZE\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why the planner sometimes ignores an index\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating indexes without blocking writes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-an-index-is\">What an index is\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An index is a separate, sorted structure (usually a B-tree) that maps column values to row locations, like the index at the back of a book. Looking up a value needs a few page reads instead of reading the whole table.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"CREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer ON orders (customer_id);\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_recent ON orders (created_at DESC);\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_open ON orders (customer_id) WHERE status = 'open';\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_customers_email ON customers (lower(email));\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE\u003c/span> INDEX idx_orders_customer \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON\u003c/span> orders (customer_id);\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE\u003c/span> INDEX idx_orders_recent \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON\u003c/span> orders (created_at \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC\u003c/span>);\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE\u003c/span> INDEX idx_orders_open \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON\u003c/span> orders (customer_id) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> status \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;open&#x27;\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">UNIQUE\u003c/span> INDEX idx_customers_email \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON\u003c/span> customers (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">lower\u003c/span>(email));\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>composite index\u003c/strong> on \u003ccode>(customer_id, created_at)\u003c/code> helps queries that filter on \u003ccode>customer_id\u003c/code>, or on both. It does not help a query that filters only on \u003ccode>created_at\u003c/code>, because order matters.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>partial index\u003c/strong> (with \u003ccode>WHERE\u003c/code>) is small and fast for a common subset.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>unique index\u003c/strong> also enforces uniqueness.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-cost\">The cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every index must be updated on every write, takes disk space and memory, and slows \u003ccode>INSERT\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>UPDATE\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>DELETE\u003c/code>. Add the indexes your queries need, and remove the ones nothing uses.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT relname, indexrelname, idx_scan\nFROM pg_stat_user_indexes\nORDER BY idx_scan ASC\nLIMIT 10;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> relname, indexrelname, idx_scan\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_user_indexes\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> idx_scan \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ASC\u003c/span>\nLIMIT \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An index with \u003ccode>idx_scan\u003c/code> of zero for a long time is a candidate for removal (check replicas and rare reports first).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-a-plan\">Reading a plan\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"EXPLAIN ANALYZE\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42 ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">EXPLAIN ANALYZE\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">*\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> orders \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> customer_id \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">42\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> created_at \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC\u003c/span> LIMIT \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">20\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"Limit  (cost=0.43..8.10 rows=20) (actual time=0.05..0.09 rows=20 loops=1)\n  -&gt;  Index Scan using idx_orders_customer on orders\n        (cost=0.43..120.5 rows=310) (actual time=0.04..0.08 rows=20 loops=1)\n        Index Cond: (customer_id = 42)\nPlanning Time: 0.2 ms\nExecution Time: 0.12 ms\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">Limit  (cost=0.43..8.10 rows=20) (actual time=0.05..0.09 rows=20 loops=1)\n  -&gt;  Index Scan using idx_orders_customer on orders\n        (cost=0.43..120.5 rows=310) (actual time=0.04..0.08 rows=20 loops=1)\n        Index Cond: (customer_id = 42)\nPlanning Time: 0.2 ms\nExecution Time: 0.12 ms\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Look for these in order:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Scan type\u003c/strong>: \u003ccode>Seq Scan\u003c/code> on a big table is a warning. \u003ccode>Index Scan\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>Index Only Scan\u003c/code> is usually good.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Estimated rows versus actual rows\u003c/strong>. A large gap means the statistics are stale. Run \u003ccode>ANALYZE\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Where the time goes\u003c/strong>: find the node with the largest actual time.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Sorts that spill to disk\u003c/strong> and nested loops over huge row counts.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>EXPLAIN\u003c/code> alone shows the plan without running the query. \u003ccode>EXPLAIN ANALYZE\u003c/code> actually runs it, so wrap writes in a transaction you roll back.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-an-index-is-not-used\">Why an index is not used\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The query returns a large share of the table, so a sequential scan is genuinely cheaper\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A function or cast on the column (\u003ccode>WHERE lower(email) = ...\u003c/code> needs an index on \u003ccode>lower(email)\u003c/code>)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A leading wildcard (\u003ccode>LIKE &#39;%abc&#39;\u003c/code>)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mismatched types, such as comparing text to a number\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stale statistics\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"adding-an-index-on-a-live-system\">Adding an index on a live system\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A plain \u003ccode>CREATE INDEX\u003c/code> blocks writes to the table while it builds. On a busy table use:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_customer ON orders (customer_id);\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE\u003c/span> INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_customer \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON\u003c/span> orders (customer_id);\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>It takes longer and cannot run inside a transaction, and a failed build leaves an invalid index that you must drop and retry. MySQL uses online DDL with \u003ccode>ALGORITHM=INPLACE\u003c/code> for similar results.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A table has an index on (customer_id, created_at). Does a query with only WHERE created_at &gt; '2025-01-01' use it efficiently?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Not efficiently. The index is sorted by customer_id first, so a filter on created_at alone cannot use it as a range seek. A separate index on created_at would help.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Column order in a composite index decides which queries it serves.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"slow&gt;plan;plan&gt;cause;cause&gt;fix\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Finding why a query is slow\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"slow\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Slow query found\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"plan\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">EXPLAIN ANALYZE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cause\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Find the cause\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fix\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Fix and measure\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Slow query found to EXPLAIN ANALYZE; 2, EXPLAIN ANALYZE to Find the cause; 3, Find the cause to Fix and measure.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"slow\">\u003cdt>Slow query found \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> From slow query log or monitoring.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Tuning random queries wastes time. Start with the worst by total time.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"plan\">\u003cdt>EXPLAIN ANALYZE \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shows scan type, row estimates and time per step.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Plain EXPLAIN hides the real timings.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cause\">\u003cdt>Find the cause \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Missing index, stale statistics, bad predicate or too many rows.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Adding an index without reading the plan may not help.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fix\">\u003cdt>Fix and measure \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Add an index concurrently or rewrite the query, then compare.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No before and after number means no proof.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-index-build-that-blocked-checkout\">Incident: the index build that blocked checkout\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>At peak traffic, a plain CREATE INDEX on the orders table blocked every insert for eleven minutes. Checkout timed out for customers.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql shop -c &quot;SELECT pid, wait_event_type, left(query,50) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE wait_event_type = 'Lock' LIMIT 3&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql shop -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT pid, wait_event_type, left(query,50) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE wait_event_type = &#x27;Lock&#x27; LIMIT 3&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team cancelled the build, re-ran it with \u003ccode>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY\u003c/code> in a quiet period, and added index creation to the migration checklist.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Indexing every column\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading only plain EXPLAIN\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Wrong column order in composite indexes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Building indexes without CONCURRENTLY at peak time\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-an-index-is","text":"What an index is"},{"id":"the-cost","text":"The cost"},{"id":"reading-a-plan","text":"Reading a plan"},{"id":"why-an-index-is-not-used","text":"Why an index is not used"},{"id":"adding-an-index-on-a-live-system","text":"Adding an index on a live system"},{"id":"incident-the-index-build-that-blocked-checkout","text":"Incident: the index build that blocked checkout"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Indexes","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes.html"},{"t":"Using EXPLAIN","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/using-explain.html"},{"t":"CREATE INDEX","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-createindex.html"}],"text":"A table with ten rows is fast however you query it. A table with fifty million rows is only fast when the database can jump straight to the rows it needs. Indexes make that possible, and query plans show whether the database is using them. What you will learn What an index is and when it helps The cost of indexes Reading EXPLAIN and EXPLAIN ANALYZE Why the planner sometimes ignores an index Creating indexes without blocking writes What an index is An index is a separate, sorted structure (usually a B-tree) that maps column values to row locations, like the index at the back of a book. Looking up a value needs a few page reads instead of reading the whole table. CREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer ON orders (customer_id); CREATE INDEX idx_orders_recent ON orders (created_at DESC ); CREATE INDEX idx_orders_open ON orders (customer_id) WHERE status = &#x27;open&#x27; ; CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_customers_email ON customers ( lower (email)); A composite index on (customer_id, created_at) helps queries that filter on customer_id , or on both. It does not help a query that filters only on created_at , because order matters. A partial index (with WHERE ) is small and fast for a common subset. A unique index also enforces uniqueness. The cost Every index must be updated on every write, takes disk space and memory, and slows INSERT , UPDATE and DELETE . Add the indexes your queries need, and remove the ones nothing uses. SELECT relname, indexrelname, idx_scan FROM pg_stat_user_indexes ORDER BY idx_scan ASC LIMIT 10 ; An index with idx_scan of zero for a long time is a candidate for removal (check replicas and rare reports first). Reading a plan EXPLAIN ANALYZE SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42 ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20 ; Limit (cost=0.43..8.10 rows=20) (actual time=0.05..0.09 rows=20 loops=1) -> Index Scan using idx_orders_customer on orders (cost=0.43..120.5 rows=310) (actual time=0.04..0.08 rows=20 loops=1) Index Cond: (customer_id = 42) Planning Time: 0.2 ms Execution Time: 0.12 ms Look for these in order: Scan type : Seq Scan on a big table is a warning. Index Scan or Index Only Scan is usually good. Estimated rows versus actual rows . A large gap means the statistics are stale. Run ANALYZE . Where the time goes : find the node with the largest actual time. Sorts that spill to disk and nested loops over huge row counts. EXPLAIN alone shows the plan without running the query. EXPLAIN ANALYZE actually runs it, so wrap writes in a transaction you roll back. Why an index is not used The query returns a large share of the table, so a sequential scan is genuinely cheaper A function or cast on the column ( WHERE lower(email) = ... needs an index on lower(email) ) A leading wildcard ( LIKE &#39;%abc&#39; ) Mismatched types, such as comparing text to a number Stale statistics Adding an index on a live system A plain CREATE INDEX blocks writes to the table while it builds. On a busy table use: CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_customer ON orders (customer_id); It takes longer and cannot run inside a transaction, and a failed build leaves an invalid index that you must drop and retry. MySQL uses online DDL with ALGORITHM=INPLACE for similar results. Predict first. A table has an index on (customer_id, created_at). Does a query with only WHERE created_at > '2025-01-01' use it efficiently? Your guess Answer: Not efficiently. The index is sorted by customer_id first, so a filter on created_at alone cannot use it as a range seek. A separate index on created_at would help. Column order in a composite index decides which queries it serves. Finding why a query is slow Flow in order: 1, Slow query found to EXPLAIN ANALYZE; 2, EXPLAIN ANALYZE to Find the cause; 3, Find the cause to Fix and measure. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Slow query found Step What it does: From slow query log or monitoring. If it fails or is weak: Tuning random queries waste"},"databases/transactions-and-locking":{"html":"\u003cp>A bank transfer must either move the money out and in, or do neither. Transactions give databases that promise. They also explain many production stalls, because a transaction that stays open too long holds locks that everyone else waits for.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>ACID in plain terms\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Isolation levels and the anomalies they prevent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Row locks, table locks and deadlocks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Finding blocking sessions and long transactions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"acid\">ACID\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Letter\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>What it protects you from\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Atomicity\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>All statements in a transaction apply, or none\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Half-done transfers\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Consistency\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Constraints hold before and after\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Orphaned or invalid rows\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Isolation\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Concurrent transactions do not see each other&#39;s partial work\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Reading unfinished data\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Durability\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Committed data survives a crash\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Losing acknowledged writes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Durability comes from the \u003cstrong>write-ahead log (WAL)\u003c/strong>: changes are written to the log and flushed to disk before the commit is acknowledged.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-transaction\">A transaction\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"BEGIN;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;\nCOMMIT;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BEGIN\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">UPDATE\u003c/span> accounts \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SET\u003c/span> balance \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> balance \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">100\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> id \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">UPDATE\u003c/span> accounts \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SET\u003c/span> balance \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> balance \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">+\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">100\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> id \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COMMIT\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If the server crashes between the two updates, neither remains after recovery.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"isolation-levels\">Isolation levels\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Level\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Prevents\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Notes\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Read committed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Reading uncommitted data\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Default in PostgreSQL. Each statement sees data committed before it began.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Repeatable read\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Rows changing between two reads in one transaction\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Default in MySQL InnoDB.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Serializable\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>All anomalies, as if transactions ran one after another\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Can abort transactions that must be retried.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Stronger isolation costs more coordination. Applications using serializable mode must be ready to retry on a serialization failure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"locks\">Locks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Writers lock the rows they change until commit or rollback. Readers normally do not block writers (PostgreSQL uses MVCC, keeping old versions visible to readers).\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE id = 1 FOR UPDATE;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">*\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> accounts \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> id \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FOR\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">UPDATE\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>FOR UPDATE\u003c/code> locks selected rows so you can safely read then update them. Schema changes (\u003ccode>ALTER TABLE\u003c/code>) take stronger table-level locks, and a waiting \u003ccode>ALTER\u003c/code> can queue behind a long transaction while blocking everyone behind it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Deadlock\u003c/strong>: transaction A holds row 1 and waits for row 2, while B holds row 2 and waits for row 1. The database detects it, aborts one transaction and reports an error. Prevent deadlocks by updating rows in a consistent order and keeping transactions short.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"find-blockers\">Find blockers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT blocked.pid AS blocked_pid, blocking.pid AS blocking_pid,\n       left(blocking.query, 60) AS blocking_query,\n       now() - blocking.xact_start AS blocking_for\nFROM pg_stat_activity blocked\nJOIN pg_stat_activity blocking\n  ON blocking.pid = ANY (pg_blocking_pids(blocked.pid));\n\nSELECT pid, state, now() - xact_start AS xact_age, left(query, 60)\nFROM pg_stat_activity\nWHERE xact_start IS NOT NULL\nORDER BY xact_age DESC LIMIT 5;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> blocked.pid \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> blocked_pid, blocking.pid \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> blocking_pid,\n       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">left\u003c/span>(blocking.query, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">60\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> blocking_query,\n       now() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">-\u003c/span> blocking.xact_start \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> blocking_for\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_activity blocked\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN\u003c/span> pg_stat_activity blocking\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON\u003c/span> blocking.pid \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ANY\u003c/span> (pg_blocking_pids(blocked.pid));\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> pid, state, now() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">-\u003c/span> xact_start \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> xact_age, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">left\u003c/span>(query, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">60\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_activity\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> xact_start \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">IS\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> xact_age \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC\u003c/span> LIMIT \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Look for sessions in state \u003ccode>idle in transaction\u003c/code>. They hold locks (and stop cleanup of old row versions) while doing nothing. Protect yourself with timeouts:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ALTER ROLE app SET idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = '30s';\nALTER ROLE app SET lock_timeout = '5s';\nALTER ROLE app SET statement_timeout = '30s';\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ALTER\u003c/span> ROLE app \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SET\u003c/span> idle_in_transaction_session_timeout \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;30s&#x27;\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ALTER\u003c/span> ROLE app \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SET\u003c/span> lock_timeout \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;5s&#x27;\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ALTER\u003c/span> ROLE app \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SET\u003c/span> statement_timeout \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;30s&#x27;\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>To end a stuck session: \u003ccode>SELECT pg_cancel_backend(pid);\u003c/code> cancels the query, and \u003ccode>pg_terminate_backend(pid)\u003c/code> ends the connection. Fix the cause in the application afterwards.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A developer opens a transaction, runs one UPDATE, and then goes to lunch without committing. What do other sessions experience?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Anyone who wants to change the same rows waits until the transaction ends, and the database cannot clean up old row versions. A lock_timeout or idle_in_transaction_session_timeout limits the damage.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Open transactions are not free. Keep them short.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"tx&gt;alter;alter&gt;pile;pile&gt;out\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How one long transaction becomes an outage\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tx\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Long open transaction\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"alter\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Schema change waits\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pile\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Queries pile up\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"out\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">User-facing outage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Long open transaction to Schema change waits; 2, Schema change waits to Queries pile up; 3, Queries pile up to User-facing outage.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tx\">\u003cdt>Long open transaction \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds row locks and an old snapshot.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Idle in transaction sessions are invisible without monitoring.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"alter\">\u003cdt>Schema change waits \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> ALTER TABLE queues for a lock behind the transaction.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The queued ALTER now blocks every new query.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pile\">\u003cdt>Queries pile up \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Connections fill with waiting statements.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The pool is exhausted and the app times out.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"out\">\u003cdt>User-facing outage \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Requests fail across the service.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Killing the wrong session may make it worse.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-migration-that-waited-for-a-lock\">Incident: the migration that waited for a lock\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A routine ALTER TABLE queued behind a report transaction that had been open for forty minutes. New queries queued behind the ALTER, and the site went down in seconds.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql shop -c &quot;SELECT pid, state, now()-xact_start AS age FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE xact_start IS NOT NULL ORDER BY age DESC LIMIT 2&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql shop -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT pid, state, now()-xact_start AS age FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE xact_start IS NOT NULL ORDER BY age DESC LIMIT 2&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>They cancelled the ALTER first, which freed the queue, set \u003ccode>lock_timeout\u003c/code> on migrations so they fail fast, and moved reports to a replica.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Holding a transaction open while calling another service\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running schema changes with no lock timeout\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring \u003ccode>idle in transaction\u003c/code> sessions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying deadlocks forever without finding the order problem\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"acid","text":"ACID"},{"id":"a-transaction","text":"A transaction"},{"id":"isolation-levels","text":"Isolation levels"},{"id":"locks","text":"Locks"},{"id":"find-blockers","text":"Find blockers"},{"id":"incident-the-migration-that-waited-for-a-lock","text":"Incident: the migration that waited for a lock"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Transaction isolation","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html"},{"t":"Explicit locking","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/explicit-locking.html"},{"t":"Monitoring database activity","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/monitoring-stats.html"}],"text":"A bank transfer must either move the money out and in, or do neither. Transactions give databases that promise. They also explain many production stalls, because a transaction that stays open too long holds locks that everyone else waits for. What you will learn ACID in plain terms Isolation levels and the anomalies they prevent Row locks, table locks and deadlocks Finding blocking sessions and long transactions ACID Letter Meaning What it protects you from Atomicity All statements in a transaction apply, or none Half-done transfers Consistency Constraints hold before and after Orphaned or invalid rows Isolation Concurrent transactions do not see each other&#39;s partial work Reading unfinished data Durability Committed data survives a crash Losing acknowledged writes Durability comes from the write-ahead log (WAL) : changes are written to the log and flushed to disk before the commit is acknowledged. A transaction BEGIN ; UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1 ; UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2 ; COMMIT ; If the server crashes between the two updates, neither remains after recovery. Isolation levels Level Prevents Notes Read committed Reading uncommitted data Default in PostgreSQL. Each statement sees data committed before it began. Repeatable read Rows changing between two reads in one transaction Default in MySQL InnoDB. Serializable All anomalies, as if transactions ran one after another Can abort transactions that must be retried. Stronger isolation costs more coordination. Applications using serializable mode must be ready to retry on a serialization failure. Locks Writers lock the rows they change until commit or rollback. Readers normally do not block writers (PostgreSQL uses MVCC, keeping old versions visible to readers). SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE id = 1 FOR UPDATE ; FOR UPDATE locks selected rows so you can safely read then update them. Schema changes ( ALTER TABLE ) take stronger table-level locks, and a waiting ALTER can queue behind a long transaction while blocking everyone behind it. Deadlock : transaction A holds row 1 and waits for row 2, while B holds row 2 and waits for row 1. The database detects it, aborts one transaction and reports an error. Prevent deadlocks by updating rows in a consistent order and keeping transactions short. Find blockers SELECT blocked.pid AS blocked_pid, blocking.pid AS blocking_pid, left (blocking.query, 60 ) AS blocking_query, now() - blocking.xact_start AS blocking_for FROM pg_stat_activity blocked JOIN pg_stat_activity blocking ON blocking.pid = ANY (pg_blocking_pids(blocked.pid)); SELECT pid, state, now() - xact_start AS xact_age, left (query, 60 ) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE xact_start IS NOT NULL ORDER BY xact_age DESC LIMIT 5 ; Look for sessions in state idle in transaction . They hold locks (and stop cleanup of old row versions) while doing nothing. Protect yourself with timeouts: ALTER ROLE app SET idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = &#x27;30s&#x27; ; ALTER ROLE app SET lock_timeout = &#x27;5s&#x27; ; ALTER ROLE app SET statement_timeout = &#x27;30s&#x27; ; To end a stuck session: SELECT pg_cancel_backend(pid); cancels the query, and pg_terminate_backend(pid) ends the connection. Fix the cause in the application afterwards. Predict first. A developer opens a transaction, runs one UPDATE, and then goes to lunch without committing. What do other sessions experience? Your guess Answer: Anyone who wants to change the same rows waits until the transaction ends, and the database cannot clean up old row versions. A lock_timeout or idle_in_transaction_session_timeout limits the damage. Open transactions are not free. Keep them short. How one long transaction becomes an outage Flow in order: 1, Long open transaction to Schema change waits; 2, Schema change waits to Queries pile up; 3, Queries pile up to User-facing outage. Step Client Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens wh"},"databases/backups-and-recovery":{"html":"\u003cp>A backup you have never restored is a hope, not a backup. Databases are the part of your system that cannot be rebuilt from code, so backup design and restore practice are core operator skills.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Logical and physical backups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Point-in-time recovery (PITR)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>RPO and RTO as targets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storing backups safely\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restore drills\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-kinds-of-backup\">Two kinds of backup\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Kind\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Good for\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Weak at\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Logical\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>pg_dump\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>mysqldump\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Small databases, moving between versions, single tables\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Slow on large data, restores rebuild indexes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Physical\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>pg_basebackup\u003c/code>, volume snapshots\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Fast restore of large databases, replicas\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Same major version and platform\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pg_dump --format=custom --file=shop.dump shop\npg_restore --dbname=shop_restored --jobs=4 shop.dump\npg_basebackup -h db1.example.com -D /backups/base -X stream -P\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">pg_dump --format=custom --file=shop.dump shop\npg_restore --dbname=shop_restored --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">jobs\u003c/span>=4 shop.dump\npg_basebackup -h db1.example.com -D /backups/base -X stream -P\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Snapshots of a disk are only safe if the database is consistent at that moment. Use the database&#39;s backup mode or a snapshot method that is crash-consistent and combine with the log archive.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"point-in-time-recovery\">Point-in-time recovery\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A base backup plus the continuous archive of the write-ahead log lets you restore to any moment, for example &quot;one minute before the bad delete&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# postgresql.conf on the primary\nwal_level = replica\narchive_mode = on\narchive_command = 'test ! -f /archive/%f &amp;&amp; cp %p /archive/%f'\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\"># postgresql.conf on the primary\nwal_level = replica\narchive_mode = on\narchive_command = &#x27;test ! -f /archive/%f &amp;&amp; cp %p /archive/%f&#x27;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# recovery settings when restoring\nrestore_command = 'cp /archive/%f %p'\nrecovery_target_time = '2026-03-04 09:41:00+00'\nrecovery_target_action = 'promote'\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\"># recovery settings when restoring\nrestore_command = &#x27;cp /archive/%f %p&#x27;\nrecovery_target_time = &#x27;2026-03-04 09:41:00+00&#x27;\nrecovery_target_action = &#x27;promote&#x27;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Real systems copy the archive to remote object storage with a tool such as pgBackRest or WAL-G rather than a plain \u003ccode>cp\u003c/code>. Managed services (RDS, Cloud SQL, Azure Database) offer PITR as a feature, but you still decide retention and test the restore.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"targets-rpo-and-rto\">Targets: RPO and RTO\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>RPO\u003c/strong> (recovery point objective): how much data you can afford to lose. With PITR and continuous archiving it is seconds to minutes. With nightly dumps it is up to 24 hours.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>RTO\u003c/strong> (recovery time objective): how long recovery may take. Restoring 2 TB takes hours, so test it.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Agree these numbers with the business first. They decide cost.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"store-them-safely\">Store them safely\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Keep copies in a different account or region than the primary\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Encrypt backups and protect the keys separately\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Make a copy immutable or write-once, so ransomware or a mistaken delete cannot erase it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Monitor that backups finished and that the archive is not falling behind\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep retention rules, and delete data you must delete under privacy rules\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\">Prove it works\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"createdb shop_check\npg_restore --dbname=shop_check --exit-on-error shop.dump\npsql shop_check -c &quot;SELECT count(*) FROM orders;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">createdb shop_check\npg_restore --dbname=shop_check --exit-on-error shop.dump\npsql shop_check -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT count(*) FROM orders;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A restore drill checks that the files are complete, the keys work, the procedure is written correctly and the time matches the RTO. Run it on a schedule and automate it where possible: restore to a scratch instance, run a few sanity queries and compare row counts, then discard it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You take a nightly dump at 02:00, and an engineer drops a table at 14:00. Using only the dump, how much data do you lose?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Everything written between 02:00 and the restore, about twelve hours. PITR with archived WAL would let you restore to 13:59 instead.\u003c/p>\u003cp>That gap is your RPO, so choose the backup method to match.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"base&gt;replay;wal&gt;replay;replay&gt;check\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Point-in-time recovery\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"base\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Base backup\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"wal\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">WAL archive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"replay\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Replay to target\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"check\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Verify and promote\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Base backup to Replay to target; 2, WAL archive to Replay to target; 3, Replay to target to Verify and promote.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"base\">\u003cdt>Base backup \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A full physical copy taken at a known time.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An untested or corrupt base makes the archive useless.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"wal\">\u003cdt>WAL archive \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A continuous stream of changes copied off the server.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A gap in the archive stops recovery at that point.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"replay\">\u003cdt>Replay to target \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Restore the base, then apply logs up to the chosen time.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Picking the wrong target time restores the bad change too.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"check\">\u003cdt>Verify and promote \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Check data, then open for writes or extract the needed rows.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Promoting before checking can cement a mistake.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-backups-that-were-empty\">Incident: the backups that were empty\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A company lost its production database to a mistaken deletion and found that none of its backup methods produced a usable copy: one failed silently for months, one had the wrong version and one was never configured.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ls -la /backups/nightly | tail -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -la /backups/nightly | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-rw-r--r-- 1 postgres postgres 0 Jan 12 02:00 shop.dump\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-rw-r--r-- 1 postgres postgres 0 Jan 13 02:00 shop.dump\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The company recovered data from a staging copy that was six hours old. Many teams have been in a similar position, and the lesson is the same: monitor backup size and age, and restore regularly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Backups on the same disk or account as the database\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never testing a restore\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alerts only on job exit codes, not on backup size or freshness\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming a replica is a backup (it replicates deletions too)\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"two-kinds-of-backup","text":"Two kinds of backup"},{"id":"point-in-time-recovery","text":"Point-in-time recovery"},{"id":"targets-rpo-and-rto","text":"Targets: RPO and RTO"},{"id":"store-them-safely","text":"Store them safely"},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works"},{"id":"incident-the-backups-that-were-empty","text":"Incident: the backups that were empty"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Backup and restore","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/backup.html"},{"t":"Continuous archiving and point-in-time recovery","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/continuous-archiving.html"},{"t":"pgBackRest","u":"https://pgbackrest.org/"}],"text":"A backup you have never restored is a hope, not a backup. Databases are the part of your system that cannot be rebuilt from code, so backup design and restore practice are core operator skills. What you will learn Logical and physical backups Point-in-time recovery (PITR) RPO and RTO as targets Storing backups safely Restore drills Two kinds of backup Kind Example Good for Weak at Logical pg_dump , mysqldump Small databases, moving between versions, single tables Slow on large data, restores rebuild indexes Physical pg_basebackup , volume snapshots Fast restore of large databases, replicas Same major version and platform pg_dump --format=custom --file=shop.dump shop pg_restore --dbname=shop_restored -- jobs =4 shop.dump pg_basebackup -h db1.example.com -D /backups/base -X stream -P Snapshots of a disk are only safe if the database is consistent at that moment. Use the database&#39;s backup mode or a snapshot method that is crash-consistent and combine with the log archive. Point-in-time recovery A base backup plus the continuous archive of the write-ahead log lets you restore to any moment, for example \"one minute before the bad delete\". # postgresql.conf on the primary wal_level = replica archive_mode = on archive_command = &#x27;test ! -f /archive/%f && cp %p /archive/%f&#x27; # recovery settings when restoring restore_command = &#x27;cp /archive/%f %p&#x27; recovery_target_time = &#x27;2026-03-04 09:41:00+00&#x27; recovery_target_action = &#x27;promote&#x27; Real systems copy the archive to remote object storage with a tool such as pgBackRest or WAL-G rather than a plain cp . Managed services (RDS, Cloud SQL, Azure Database) offer PITR as a feature, but you still decide retention and test the restore. Targets: RPO and RTO RPO (recovery point objective): how much data you can afford to lose. With PITR and continuous archiving it is seconds to minutes. With nightly dumps it is up to 24 hours. RTO (recovery time objective): how long recovery may take. Restoring 2 TB takes hours, so test it. Agree these numbers with the business first. They decide cost. Store them safely Keep copies in a different account or region than the primary Encrypt backups and protect the keys separately Make a copy immutable or write-once, so ransomware or a mistaken delete cannot erase it Monitor that backups finished and that the archive is not falling behind Keep retention rules, and delete data you must delete under privacy rules Prove it works createdb shop_check pg_restore --dbname=shop_check --exit-on-error shop.dump psql shop_check -c \"SELECT count(*) FROM orders;\" A restore drill checks that the files are complete, the keys work, the procedure is written correctly and the time matches the RTO. Run it on a schedule and automate it where possible: restore to a scratch instance, run a few sanity queries and compare row counts, then discard it. Predict first. You take a nightly dump at 02:00, and an engineer drops a table at 14:00. Using only the dump, how much data do you lose? Your guess Answer: Everything written between 02:00 and the restore, about twelve hours. PITR with archived WAL would let you restore to 13:59 instead. That gap is your RPO, so choose the backup method to match. Point-in-time recovery Flow in order: 1, Base backup to Replay to target; 2, WAL archive to Replay to target; 3, Replay to target to Verify and promote. Data store Service Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Base backup Data store What it does: A full physical copy taken at a known time. If it fails or is weak: An untested or corrupt base makes the archive useless. WAL archive Data store What it does: A continuous stream of changes copied off the server. If it fails or is weak: A gap in the archive stops recovery at that point. Replay to target Service What it does: Restore the base, then apply logs up to the chosen time. If it fails or is weak: Picking the wrong target time restores"},"databases/replication-and-failover":{"html":"\u003cp>One database server is a single point of failure. Replication keeps copies on other machines so you can survive a failure, spread read load, and do maintenance with little downtime. It also introduces new problems: lag, failover and the risk of two servers both believing they are in charge.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Primary and replica roles\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Asynchronous and synchronous replication\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Replication lag and stale reads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Failover and split brain\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Managed high availability\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"primary-and-replicas\">Primary and replicas\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>primary\u003c/strong> accepts writes. \u003cstrong>Replicas\u003c/strong> receive the stream of changes (the write-ahead log in PostgreSQL, the binary log in MySQL) and apply them. Replicas can serve read-only queries and can be promoted to primary.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"asynchronous-or-synchronous\">Asynchronous or synchronous\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Mode\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Commit returns when\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Trade-off\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Asynchronous\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The primary has written locally\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Fast, but a primary crash can lose the last few commits\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Synchronous\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>At least one replica has confirmed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No acknowledged commit lost, but slower and a stuck replica can stall writes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Many teams use synchronous replication to a nearby replica for durability and asynchronous replicas elsewhere for reads and disaster recovery. Choose based on the RPO you agreed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"lag\">Lag\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Replicas apply changes after the primary, so reads from a replica may be slightly old. That is \u003cstrong>replication lag\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT now() - pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp() AS replay_delay;\nSELECT client_addr, state, sent_lsn, replay_lsn,\n       pg_wal_lsn_diff(sent_lsn, replay_lsn) AS bytes_behind\nFROM pg_stat_replication;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> now() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">-\u003c/span> pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> replay_delay;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> client_addr, state, sent_lsn, replay_lsn,\n       pg_wal_lsn_diff(sent_lsn, replay_lsn) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> bytes_behind\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_replication;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Causes of lag: heavy writes, a slow replica disk, long queries on the replica that conflict with applying changes, or network limits. Effects include a user saving a profile and not seeing it on the next page. Solutions include reading from the primary right after a write, session stickiness, or waiting for the replica to reach a known position.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failover\">Failover\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When the primary fails, a replica must be promoted and clients must follow.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pg_ctl promote -D /var/lib/postgresql/16/main\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">pg_ctl promote -D /var/lib/postgresql/16/main\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Doing it by hand is slow and error prone, so teams use an automated manager such as Patroni, or a managed service. A safe failover needs:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>A reliable decision that the primary is really down (a majority of a consensus group such as etcd, not one health check)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Fencing\u003c/strong>: making sure the old primary cannot accept writes again\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Promoting the most up-to-date replica\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pointing clients at the new primary through DNS, a virtual IP or a proxy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rebuilding the old primary as a replica\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Split brain\u003c/strong> happens when two servers both accept writes, causing divergent data that is hard or impossible to merge. Fencing and quorum exist to prevent it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"managed-services\">Managed services\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cloud databases offer multi-zone standby instances with automatic failover, typically within a minute or two, and read replicas as a separate feature. You still need to test failover, because connection strings, pools and application retry logic decide how long users notice an outage.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Your application writes to the primary and immediately reads from an asynchronous replica. A user sometimes does not see the data they just saved. Why?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Replication lag. The replica has not applied the change yet. Read from the primary after a write, or wait until the replica has caught up.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Replicas give eventual, not instant, consistency.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"fail&gt;elect;elect&gt;promote;promote&gt;redirect\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A failover\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fail\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Primary fails\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"elect\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Decide and fence\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"promote\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Promote a replica\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"redirect\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Redirect clients\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Primary fails to Decide and fence; 2, Decide and fence to Promote a replica; 3, Promote a replica to Redirect clients.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fail\">\u003cdt>Primary fails \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Hardware, network or process failure.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A false alarm may trigger an unneeded failover.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"elect\">\u003cdt>Decide and fence \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A quorum agrees, and the old primary is cut off.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without fencing, two primaries can accept writes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"promote\">\u003cdt>Promote a replica \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The most up-to-date replica becomes the primary.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A lagging replica loses recent commits.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"redirect\">\u003cdt>Redirect clients \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> DNS, a virtual IP or proxy moves traffic.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cached DNS and old connections keep going to the failed node.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-failover-that-made-it-worse\">Incident: the failover that made it worse\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A short network partition made an automated tool promote a replica while the old primary was still running. For several minutes both accepted writes, and the team had to reconcile the data by hand.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"patronictl list\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>patronictl list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| Member | Role   | State   | Lag in MB |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| db1    | Leader | running |           |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">| db2    | Leader | running |           |\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A well-known public example is the October 2018 incident at a large code-hosting company, which wrote up a failover after a short network partition. In general, the repair was slow, data on both sides had to be compared, and the lasting fixes were better quorum rules, fencing and tested failover procedures.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating a replica as a backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Failing over without fencing the old primary\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never rehearsing failover\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading from replicas for data that must be current\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"primary-and-replicas","text":"Primary and replicas"},{"id":"asynchronous-or-synchronous","text":"Asynchronous or synchronous"},{"id":"lag","text":"Lag"},{"id":"failover","text":"Failover"},{"id":"managed-services","text":"Managed services"},{"id":"incident-the-failover-that-made-it-worse","text":"Incident: the failover that made it worse"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"High availability, load balancing and replication","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/high-availability.html"},{"t":"Log-shipping standby servers","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/warm-standby.html"},{"t":"Patroni","u":"https://patroni.readthedocs.io/"}],"text":"One database server is a single point of failure. Replication keeps copies on other machines so you can survive a failure, spread read load, and do maintenance with little downtime. It also introduces new problems: lag, failover and the risk of two servers both believing they are in charge. What you will learn Primary and replica roles Asynchronous and synchronous replication Replication lag and stale reads Failover and split brain Managed high availability Primary and replicas The primary accepts writes. Replicas receive the stream of changes (the write-ahead log in PostgreSQL, the binary log in MySQL) and apply them. Replicas can serve read-only queries and can be promoted to primary. Asynchronous or synchronous Mode Commit returns when Trade-off Asynchronous The primary has written locally Fast, but a primary crash can lose the last few commits Synchronous At least one replica has confirmed No acknowledged commit lost, but slower and a stuck replica can stall writes Many teams use synchronous replication to a nearby replica for durability and asynchronous replicas elsewhere for reads and disaster recovery. Choose based on the RPO you agreed. Lag Replicas apply changes after the primary, so reads from a replica may be slightly old. That is replication lag . SELECT now() - pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp() AS replay_delay; SELECT client_addr, state, sent_lsn, replay_lsn, pg_wal_lsn_diff(sent_lsn, replay_lsn) AS bytes_behind FROM pg_stat_replication; Causes of lag: heavy writes, a slow replica disk, long queries on the replica that conflict with applying changes, or network limits. Effects include a user saving a profile and not seeing it on the next page. Solutions include reading from the primary right after a write, session stickiness, or waiting for the replica to reach a known position. Failover When the primary fails, a replica must be promoted and clients must follow. pg_ctl promote -D /var/lib/postgresql/16/main Doing it by hand is slow and error prone, so teams use an automated manager such as Patroni, or a managed service. A safe failover needs: A reliable decision that the primary is really down (a majority of a consensus group such as etcd, not one health check) Fencing : making sure the old primary cannot accept writes again Promoting the most up-to-date replica Pointing clients at the new primary through DNS, a virtual IP or a proxy Rebuilding the old primary as a replica Split brain happens when two servers both accept writes, causing divergent data that is hard or impossible to merge. Fencing and quorum exist to prevent it. Managed services Cloud databases offer multi-zone standby instances with automatic failover, typically within a minute or two, and read replicas as a separate feature. You still need to test failover, because connection strings, pools and application retry logic decide how long users notice an outage. Predict first. Your application writes to the primary and immediately reads from an asynchronous replica. A user sometimes does not see the data they just saved. Why? Your guess Answer: Replication lag. The replica has not applied the change yet. Read from the primary after a write, or wait until the replica has caught up. Replicas give eventual, not instant, consistency. A failover Flow in order: 1, Primary fails to Decide and fence; 2, Decide and fence to Promote a replica; 3, Promote a replica to Redirect clients. Step Client Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Primary fails Step What it does: Hardware, network or process failure. If it fails or is weak: A false alarm may trigger an unneeded failover. Decide and fence Step What it does: A quorum agrees, and the old primary is cut off. If it fails or is weak: Without fencing, two primaries can accept writes. Promote a replica Step What it does: The most up-to-date replica becomes the primary. If it fails or is weak: A lagging replica loses recent commits. Redirect cli"},"databases/performance-and-connection-pooling":{"html":"\u003cp>When a service slows down, the database is often involved, but guessing wastes hours. A few views and settings tell you whether the problem is bad queries, too many connections, memory pressure or maintenance falling behind.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Finding the most expensive queries\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cache hit ratio and memory settings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why connections are expensive, and what a pooler does\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Vacuum, bloat and statistics\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"find-the-worst-queries\">Find the worst queries\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Enable the \u003ccode>pg_stat_statements\u003c/code> extension. It records time and calls per normalised query.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT calls, round(total_exec_time::numeric, 0) AS total_ms,\n       round(mean_exec_time::numeric, 2) AS mean_ms, left(query, 70) AS query\nFROM pg_stat_statements\nORDER BY total_exec_time DESC\nLIMIT 10;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> calls, round(total_exec_time::\u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">numeric\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> total_ms,\n       round(mean_exec_time::\u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">numeric\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> mean_ms, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">left\u003c/span>(query, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">70\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> query\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_statements\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> total_exec_time \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC\u003c/span>\nLIMIT \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Sort by \u003cstrong>total\u003c/strong> time first. A query that takes 5 ms but runs 10 million times costs more than one that takes 2 seconds once a day. MySQL has the slow query log and the Performance Schema for the same job. Take the worst few to \u003ccode>EXPLAIN ANALYZE\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"memory-and-cache\">Memory and cache\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Databases are fast when working data fits in memory.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT round(100.0 * sum(blks_hit) / nullif(sum(blks_hit) + sum(blks_read), 0), 2) AS cache_hit_pct\nFROM pg_stat_database;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> round(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">100.0\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">*\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(blks_hit) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">/\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">nullif\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(blks_hit) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">+\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(blks_read), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> cache_hit_pct\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_database;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A persistently low hit ratio on an OLTP database suggests too little memory or queries that scan too much. Key PostgreSQL settings:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>shared_buffers\u003c/code>: the database&#39;s own cache, commonly around a quarter of RAM\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>work_mem\u003c/code>: memory per sort or hash \u003cstrong>per operation\u003c/strong>, so a large value times many sessions can exhaust RAM\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>effective_cache_size\u003c/code>: a hint about how much memory the OS cache provides\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Treat default settings as a starting point and change one thing at a time with measurements.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"connections-are-expensive\">Connections are expensive\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each PostgreSQL connection is a separate process that uses memory. Hundreds of idle connections from many application instances waste resources and make the server slow. A \u003cstrong>connection pooler\u003c/strong> such as PgBouncer sits in front and shares a small number of real connections among many clients.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"[pgbouncer]\npool_mode = transaction\nmax_client_conn = 2000\ndefault_pool_size = 20\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">[pgbouncer]\npool_mode = transaction\nmax_client_conn = 2000\ndefault_pool_size = 20\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>transaction\u003c/code> mode returns a server connection to the pool after each transaction, which gives the best sharing but breaks features that rely on session state\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Size the pool near the number of CPU cores times two or so, not the number of clients\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Check also the connection pool in your application framework. Many pools times many instances can still overload the database.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT state, count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY state;\nSHOW max_connections;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> state, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">count\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">*\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_activity \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> state;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SHOW\u003c/span> max_connections;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"vacuum-and-bloat\">Vacuum and bloat\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>PostgreSQL does not overwrite rows. Updates and deletes leave old row versions that \u003cstrong>autovacuum\u003c/strong> removes later. If vacuum falls behind, tables and indexes grow (\u003cstrong>bloat\u003c/strong>) and queries slow. Long transactions prevent cleanup, which is another reason to keep them short.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT relname, n_dead_tup, last_autovacuum\nFROM pg_stat_user_tables\nORDER BY n_dead_tup DESC LIMIT 5;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> relname, n_dead_tup, last_autovacuum\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_user_tables\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> n_dead_tup \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC\u003c/span> LIMIT \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Vacuum also freezes old transaction IDs. If that is blocked for too long, the database can stop accepting writes to avoid wraparound, a failure that has hit well-known services.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"quick-triage-order\">Quick triage order\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>CPU, memory, disk latency and disk space on the server\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Connection count and waiting sessions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Top queries by total time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Locks and long transactions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Replication lag and vacuum progress\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Latency rises and max_connections is nearly reached, but CPU is low. Most sessions are idle. What is the likely fix?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Too many connections held by application instances. Add a pooler such as PgBouncer or reduce the per-instance pool size, rather than raising max_connections.\u003c/p>\u003cp>More connections means more memory and context switching, not more throughput.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"host&gt;conn;host&gt;query;conn&gt;maint;query&gt;maint;maint&gt;act\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Slow database triage\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"host\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Host resources\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"conn\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Connections\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"query\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Top queries\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"maint\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Locks and vacuum\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"act\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Change one thing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Host resources to Connections; 2, Host resources to Top queries; 3, Connections to Locks and vacuum; 4, Top queries to Locks and vacuum; 5, Locks and vacuum to Change one thing.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"host\">\u003cdt>Host resources \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> CPU, memory, disk latency, free space.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A full or slow disk makes every query slow.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"conn\">\u003cdt>Connections \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Count, state and waiting sessions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Exhausted connections cause timeouts with low CPU.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"query\">\u003cdt>Top queries \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> pg_stat_statements sorted by total time.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Fixing a rare slow query ignores the frequent cheap one.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"maint\">\u003cdt>Locks and vacuum \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Long transactions, blocked sessions, dead rows.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Bloat grows quietly until it hurts.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"act\">\u003cdt>Change one thing \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Add an index, pool, tune or rewrite, then measure.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Several changes at once hide what worked.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-connection-storm\">Incident: the connection storm\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a deploy, forty new application instances each opened a pool of fifty connections. The database ran out of memory and every service using it slowed down.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;SELECT count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> count\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  1987\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team reduced each pool to five connections, placed PgBouncer in front and added an alert on connection use. They also made the deploy tool compute pool sizes from a shared connection budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Raising max_connections instead of pooling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting work_mem huge for all sessions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Looking only at the slowest single query\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring autovacuum warnings\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"find-the-worst-queries","text":"Find the worst queries"},{"id":"memory-and-cache","text":"Memory and cache"},{"id":"connections-are-expensive","text":"Connections are expensive"},{"id":"vacuum-and-bloat","text":"Vacuum and bloat"},{"id":"quick-triage-order","text":"Quick triage order"},{"id":"incident-the-connection-storm","text":"Incident: the connection storm"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"pg_stat_statements","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/pgstatstatements.html"},{"t":"Routine vacuuming","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/routine-vacuuming.html"},{"t":"PgBouncer configuration","u":"https://www.pgbouncer.org/config.html"}],"text":"When a service slows down, the database is often involved, but guessing wastes hours. A few views and settings tell you whether the problem is bad queries, too many connections, memory pressure or maintenance falling behind. What you will learn Finding the most expensive queries Cache hit ratio and memory settings Why connections are expensive, and what a pooler does Vacuum, bloat and statistics Find the worst queries Enable the pg_stat_statements extension. It records time and calls per normalised query. SELECT calls, round(total_exec_time:: numeric , 0 ) AS total_ms, round(mean_exec_time:: numeric , 2 ) AS mean_ms, left (query, 70 ) AS query FROM pg_stat_statements ORDER BY total_exec_time DESC LIMIT 10 ; Sort by total time first. A query that takes 5 ms but runs 10 million times costs more than one that takes 2 seconds once a day. MySQL has the slow query log and the Performance Schema for the same job. Take the worst few to EXPLAIN ANALYZE . Memory and cache Databases are fast when working data fits in memory. SELECT round( 100.0 * sum (blks_hit) / nullif ( sum (blks_hit) + sum (blks_read), 0 ), 2 ) AS cache_hit_pct FROM pg_stat_database; A persistently low hit ratio on an OLTP database suggests too little memory or queries that scan too much. Key PostgreSQL settings: shared_buffers : the database&#39;s own cache, commonly around a quarter of RAM work_mem : memory per sort or hash per operation , so a large value times many sessions can exhaust RAM effective_cache_size : a hint about how much memory the OS cache provides Treat default settings as a starting point and change one thing at a time with measurements. Connections are expensive Each PostgreSQL connection is a separate process that uses memory. Hundreds of idle connections from many application instances waste resources and make the server slow. A connection pooler such as PgBouncer sits in front and shares a small number of real connections among many clients. [pgbouncer] pool_mode = transaction max_client_conn = 2000 default_pool_size = 20 transaction mode returns a server connection to the pool after each transaction, which gives the best sharing but breaks features that rely on session state Size the pool near the number of CPU cores times two or so, not the number of clients Check also the connection pool in your application framework. Many pools times many instances can still overload the database. SELECT state, count ( * ) FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY state; SHOW max_connections; Vacuum and bloat PostgreSQL does not overwrite rows. Updates and deletes leave old row versions that autovacuum removes later. If vacuum falls behind, tables and indexes grow ( bloat ) and queries slow. Long transactions prevent cleanup, which is another reason to keep them short. SELECT relname, n_dead_tup, last_autovacuum FROM pg_stat_user_tables ORDER BY n_dead_tup DESC LIMIT 5 ; Vacuum also freezes old transaction IDs. If that is blocked for too long, the database can stop accepting writes to avoid wraparound, a failure that has hit well-known services. Quick triage order CPU, memory, disk latency and disk space on the server Connection count and waiting sessions Top queries by total time Locks and long transactions Replication lag and vacuum progress Predict first. Latency rises and max_connections is nearly reached, but CPU is low. Most sessions are idle. What is the likely fix? Your guess Answer: Too many connections held by application instances. Add a pooler such as PgBouncer or reduce the per-instance pool size, rather than raising max_connections. More connections means more memory and context switching, not more throughput. Slow database triage Flow in order: 1, Host resources to Connections; 2, Host resources to Top queries; 3, Connections to Locks and vacuum; 4, Top queries to Locks and vacuum; 5, Locks and vacuum to Change one thing. Service Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Host r"},"databases/safe-schema-migrations":{"html":"\u003cp>Application code can be rolled back in seconds. A schema change on a table with a hundred million rows may lock it for minutes and cannot be undone by redeploying. Safe migrations are a skill of their own.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Migration tools and versioned changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Which operations take dangerous locks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The expand and contract pattern\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backfilling data in batches\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Checks before running a migration in production\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"versioned-migrations\">Versioned migrations\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep every schema change as a numbered file in version control, applied in order by a tool (Flyway, Liquibase, Alembic, Rails or Django migrations, or Atlas). The tool records what has run in a table, so every environment ends in the same state and reviewers see the change before it ships.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"-- 0042_add_orders_status.sql\nALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN status text;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">-- 0042_add_orders_status.sql\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ALTER TABLE\u003c/span> orders \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ADD\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COLUMN\u003c/span> status text;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run migrations from the pipeline, not from a laptop, and run them before the code that needs them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"operations-that-hurt\">Operations that hurt\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Operation\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Risk on a large table\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Safer approach\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Add a column with a volatile default (older versions)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Rewrites the whole table\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Add nullable, backfill in batches, then set the default\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Add \u003ccode>NOT NULL\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Scans the table under a strong lock\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Add a \u003ccode>CHECK ... NOT VALID\u003c/code> constraint, validate separately\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Add a foreign key\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Locks and validates both tables\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Add \u003ccode>NOT VALID\u003c/code>, then \u003ccode>VALIDATE CONSTRAINT\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Create an index\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Blocks writes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Change a column type\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Rewrites the table\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Add a new column and migrate\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Rename a column\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Breaks running code\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Expand and contract\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Always set a lock timeout, so a migration that cannot get its lock fails fast instead of queueing:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SET lock_timeout = '3s';\nSET statement_timeout = '60s';\nALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN status text;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SET\u003c/span> lock_timeout \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;3s&#x27;\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SET\u003c/span> statement_timeout \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;60s&#x27;\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ALTER TABLE\u003c/span> orders \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ADD\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COLUMN\u003c/span> status text;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Retry in a quiet moment if it fails.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"expand-and-contract\">Expand and contract\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>To rename \u003ccode>customer_name\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>full_name\u003c/code> without downtime, do it in separate deploys:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Expand\u003c/strong>: add the new column \u003ccode>full_name\u003c/code> (nullable)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Write both\u003c/strong>: deploy code that writes the old and the new column\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Backfill\u003c/strong>: copy existing data in batches\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Read the new column\u003c/strong>: deploy code that reads \u003ccode>full_name\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Stop writing the old column\u003c/strong>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Contract\u003c/strong>: drop \u003ccode>customer_name\u003c/code> in a later release, after a safety period\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Each step is backward compatible with the previous version of the code, so a rollback is always possible until the final drop.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"backfill-in-batches\">Backfill in batches\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"UPDATE orders SET full_name = customer_name\nWHERE id BETWEEN 1 AND 10000 AND full_name IS NULL;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">UPDATE\u003c/span> orders \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SET\u003c/span> full_name \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> customer_name\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> id \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BETWEEN\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10000\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND\u003c/span> full_name \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">IS\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NULL\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"for start in $(seq 1 10000 1000000); do\n  psql shop -c &quot;UPDATE orders SET full_name = customer_name WHERE id BETWEEN $start AND $((start + 9999)) AND full_name IS NULL&quot;\n  sleep 0.5\ndone\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> start \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> $(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">seq\u003c/span> 1 10000 1000000); \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span>\n  psql shop -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;UPDATE orders SET full_name = customer_name WHERE id BETWEEN \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$start\u003c/span> AND \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">$((start + 9999)\u003c/span>) AND full_name IS NULL&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sleep\u003c/span> 0.5\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Small batches keep transactions short, avoid replication lag spikes, and can be stopped and resumed. Watch replica lag while it runs and slow down if it grows.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"before-production\">Before production\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Rehearse on a restored copy of production-sized data and record how long it takes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Review the lock each statement takes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Have a rollback or forward-fix plan\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Take a fresh backup or know your PITR window\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Do not combine a risky migration with a code release in the same deploy\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You must make a nullable column NOT NULL on a table with 200 million rows. What is a safer approach than ALTER COLUMN SET NOT NULL directly?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Add a CHECK (column IS NOT NULL) constraint as NOT VALID, which is quick, then run VALIDATE CONSTRAINT, which does not block writes for long. On recent PostgreSQL versions the validated constraint can then be used to set NOT NULL cheaply.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Splitting a change into a quick lock and a slow non-blocking check keeps the site up.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"exp&gt;dual;dual&gt;read;read&gt;drop\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Expand and contract\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"exp\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Expand\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dual\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Write both\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"read\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Read new\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"drop\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Contract\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Expand to Write both; 2, Write both to Read new; 3, Read new to Contract.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"exp\">\u003cdt>Expand \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Add the new column or table, nullable.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Adding it with a heavy default locks the table.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dual\">\u003cdt>Write both \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Code writes old and new, then data is backfilled.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forgetting rows written before dual-write leaves gaps.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"read\">\u003cdt>Read new \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Switch reads to the new column.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Switching before the backfill finishes returns missing data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"drop\">\u003cdt>Contract \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Drop the old column after a safe period.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Dropping too early removes your rollback.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-column-rename\">Incident: the column rename\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A single deploy renamed a column and shipped the code that used the new name. During the rolling update, old application instances still queried the old name and returned errors for seven minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl logs deploy/shop | grep -c 'column &quot;customer_name&quot; does not exist'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs deploy/shop | grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;column &quot;customer_name&quot; does not exist&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">18342\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team adopted expand and contract for all renames, added a rule that migrations must work with both the old and new code versions, and tested that rule in staging by running the previous release against the new schema.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running a table rewrite at peak time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Migration and code in one irreversible step\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No lock timeout\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping a rehearsal on realistic data\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"versioned-migrations","text":"Versioned migrations"},{"id":"operations-that-hurt","text":"Operations that hurt"},{"id":"expand-and-contract","text":"Expand and contract"},{"id":"backfill-in-batches","text":"Backfill in batches"},{"id":"before-production","text":"Before production"},{"id":"incident-the-column-rename","text":"Incident: the column rename"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"ALTER TABLE","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-altertable.html"},{"t":"CREATE INDEX","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-createindex.html"},{"t":"MySQL online DDL","u":"https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/innodb-online-ddl.html"}],"text":"Application code can be rolled back in seconds. A schema change on a table with a hundred million rows may lock it for minutes and cannot be undone by redeploying. Safe migrations are a skill of their own. What you will learn Migration tools and versioned changes Which operations take dangerous locks The expand and contract pattern Backfilling data in batches Checks before running a migration in production Versioned migrations Keep every schema change as a numbered file in version control, applied in order by a tool (Flyway, Liquibase, Alembic, Rails or Django migrations, or Atlas). The tool records what has run in a table, so every environment ends in the same state and reviewers see the change before it ships. -- 0042_add_orders_status.sql ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN status text; Run migrations from the pipeline, not from a laptop, and run them before the code that needs them. Operations that hurt Operation Risk on a large table Safer approach Add a column with a volatile default (older versions) Rewrites the whole table Add nullable, backfill in batches, then set the default Add NOT NULL Scans the table under a strong lock Add a CHECK ... NOT VALID constraint, validate separately Add a foreign key Locks and validates both tables Add NOT VALID , then VALIDATE CONSTRAINT Create an index Blocks writes CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY Change a column type Rewrites the table Add a new column and migrate Rename a column Breaks running code Expand and contract Always set a lock timeout, so a migration that cannot get its lock fails fast instead of queueing: SET lock_timeout = &#x27;3s&#x27; ; SET statement_timeout = &#x27;60s&#x27; ; ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN status text; Retry in a quiet moment if it fails. Expand and contract To rename customer_name to full_name without downtime, do it in separate deploys: Expand : add the new column full_name (nullable) Write both : deploy code that writes the old and the new column Backfill : copy existing data in batches Read the new column : deploy code that reads full_name Stop writing the old column Contract : drop customer_name in a later release, after a safety period Each step is backward compatible with the previous version of the code, so a rollback is always possible until the final drop. Backfill in batches UPDATE orders SET full_name = customer_name WHERE id BETWEEN 1 AND 10000 AND full_name IS NULL ; for start in $( seq 1 10000 1000000); do psql shop -c \"UPDATE orders SET full_name = customer_name WHERE id BETWEEN $start AND $((start + 9999) ) AND full_name IS NULL\" sleep 0.5 done Small batches keep transactions short, avoid replication lag spikes, and can be stopped and resumed. Watch replica lag while it runs and slow down if it grows. Before production Rehearse on a restored copy of production-sized data and record how long it takes Review the lock each statement takes Have a rollback or forward-fix plan Take a fresh backup or know your PITR window Do not combine a risky migration with a code release in the same deploy Predict first. You must make a nullable column NOT NULL on a table with 200 million rows. What is a safer approach than ALTER COLUMN SET NOT NULL directly? Your guess Answer: Add a CHECK (column IS NOT NULL) constraint as NOT VALID, which is quick, then run VALIDATE CONSTRAINT, which does not block writes for long. On recent PostgreSQL versions the validated constraint can then be used to set NOT NULL cheaply. Splitting a change into a quick lock and a slow non-blocking check keeps the site up. Expand and contract Flow in order: 1, Expand to Write both; 2, Write both to Read new; 3, Read new to Contract. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Expand Step What it does: Add the new column or table, nullable. If it fails or is weak: Adding it with a heavy default locks the table. Write both Step What it does: Code writes old and new, then data is backfilled. If it fails or is weak: Forget"},"databases/nosql-caches-and-choosing":{"html":"\u003cp>Relational databases are the right default, but not the only tool. Operators meet caches, queues, document stores and managed key-value services, and each has distinct failure modes. Choosing well starts from the access pattern, not from fashion.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The main families of data stores\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The CAP trade-off and eventual consistency in plain terms\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Caching patterns and cache failure modes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Operating Redis safely\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A checklist for choosing\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"families\">Families\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Family\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Examples\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Good at\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Watch out for\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Relational\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>PostgreSQL, MySQL\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Joins, constraints, transactions\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Scaling writes beyond one node\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Key-value\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Redis, DynamoDB\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Fast lookups by key, huge scale\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Limited queries\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Document\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MongoDB\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Flexible records, nested data\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Consistency across documents\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Wide-column\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Cassandra\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>High write rates across regions\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Query design must come first\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Search\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Elasticsearch, OpenSearch\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Full text and filtering\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Not a primary source of truth\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Time series\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Prometheus, TimescaleDB\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Metrics over time\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>High cardinality cost\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"consistency-in-plain-terms\">Consistency in plain terms\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When data is spread over several machines, a network partition forces a choice: keep answering (\u003cstrong>availability\u003c/strong>) and risk stale answers, or refuse some requests to stay correct (\u003cstrong>consistency\u003c/strong>). This is the idea behind CAP. Many stores offer \u003cstrong>eventual consistency\u003c/strong>: replicas converge shortly after a write, but a read right away may be stale. Some let you pick per request, for instance strongly consistent reads at higher cost.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"caching\">Caching\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A cache keeps copies of expensive results in fast memory. Common patterns:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Cache-aside\u003c/strong>: the application checks the cache, on a miss reads the database and writes the result back with an expiry (TTL)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Write-through\u003c/strong>: writes go to the cache and the database together\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Invalidate on change\u003c/strong>: delete or update the cached item when the source changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Failure modes to plan for:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Cold cache\u003c/strong>: after a restart every request misses and hits the database at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Stampede\u003c/strong>: a popular key expires and thousands of requests rebuild it together. Add jitter to TTLs and let only one request rebuild.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Stale data\u003c/strong>: choose TTLs deliberately and invalidate on writes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Cache as the only copy\u003c/strong>: if you cannot rebuild it, it is a database and needs backups and persistence\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"operating-redis-safely\">Operating Redis safely\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"redis-cli INFO memory | grep -E &quot;used_memory_human|maxmemory_human&quot;\nredis-cli INFO stats | grep -E &quot;keyspace_hits|keyspace_misses|evicted_keys&quot;\nredis-cli --scan --pattern 'session:*' | head\nredis-cli SLOWLOG GET 5\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">redis-cli INFO memory | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;used_memory_human|maxmemory_human&quot;\u003c/span>\nredis-cli INFO stats | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;keyspace_hits|keyspace_misses|evicted_keys&quot;\u003c/span>\nredis-cli --scan --pattern \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;session:*&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\nredis-cli SLOWLOG GET 5\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"maxmemory 2gb\nmaxmemory-policy allkeys-lru\nappendonly yes\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">maxmemory 2gb\nmaxmemory-policy allkeys-lru\nappendonly yes\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Set \u003ccode>maxmemory\u003c/code> and an eviction policy, or Redis will grow until the host runs out of memory\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never run \u003ccode>KEYS *\u003c/code> on production, because it blocks the single-threaded server. Use \u003ccode>SCAN\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Persistence (\u003ccode>appendonly\u003c/code>, snapshots) is optional for a pure cache and important for queues or sessions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Do not expose Redis to the internet. Use a private network, authentication and TLS.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-to-choose\">How to choose\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Ask in order:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>What are the main queries and how often? Start from reads and writes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Do you need transactions and joins across entities? Pick relational.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How much data and what write rate in a year? Does one node suffice?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What consistency can users tolerate?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Who will operate it, and do you have backups, monitoring and skills for it?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is a managed service available?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>A boring relational database plus a cache covers a very large share of real systems. Adding another store adds a new thing to back up, secure, monitor and patch.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A popular product page is cached for 60 seconds. At the moment it expires, 5,000 requests arrive. What can happen and how do you reduce it?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> All 5,000 miss at once and hit the database to rebuild the same page, a cache stampede. Use a lock so one request rebuilds, serve slightly stale data while refreshing, and add random jitter to TTLs.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A cache protects the database only while it is warm.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"app&gt;cache;app&gt;db;db&gt;fill;fill&gt;cache\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Cache-aside read path\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"83.3\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-cache\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cache\" data-kind=\"cache\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cache\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Cache\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"db\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Database\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-cache\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fill\" data-kind=\"cache\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Store with TTL\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Cache\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Application to Cache; 2, Application to Database; 3, Database to Store with TTL; 4, Store with TTL to Cache.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-cache\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Cache\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Asks the cache first.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A cache outage must not take the app down.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cache\">\u003cdt>Cache \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Cache\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Returns a hit, or a miss on absent or expired keys.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Eviction and expiry cause sudden misses.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"db\">\u003cdt>Database \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Source of truth for a miss.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A mass miss can overload it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fill\">\u003cdt>Store with TTL \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Cache\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The result is written back with an expiry.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A long TTL serves stale data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-restart-that-melted-the-database\">Incident: the restart that melted the database\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>During maintenance a team restarted its Redis cache. Every request missed, the database received the full traffic it normally never saw, and it fell over within a minute.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"redis-cli INFO stats | grep keyspace\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>redis-cli INFO stats | grep keyspace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">keyspace_hits:0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">keyspace_misses:482113\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>They added gradual cache warming, rate-limited the rebuilds, replicas for the cache, and load tested the database with the cache turned off to learn its real limits.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choosing a store because it is popular\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating a cache as durable storage\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running KEYS on production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No memory limit or eviction policy\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"families","text":"Families"},{"id":"consistency-in-plain-terms","text":"Consistency in plain terms"},{"id":"caching","text":"Caching"},{"id":"operating-redis-safely","text":"Operating Redis safely"},{"id":"how-to-choose","text":"How to choose"},{"id":"incident-the-restart-that-melted-the-database","text":"Incident: the restart that melted the database"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Redis documentation","u":"https://redis.io/docs/latest/"},{"t":"MongoDB manual","u":"https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/"},{"t":"Amazon DynamoDB developer guide","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Introduction.html"}],"text":"Relational databases are the right default, but not the only tool. Operators meet caches, queues, document stores and managed key-value services, and each has distinct failure modes. Choosing well starts from the access pattern, not from fashion. What you will learn The main families of data stores The CAP trade-off and eventual consistency in plain terms Caching patterns and cache failure modes Operating Redis safely A checklist for choosing Families Family Examples Good at Watch out for Relational PostgreSQL, MySQL Joins, constraints, transactions Scaling writes beyond one node Key-value Redis, DynamoDB Fast lookups by key, huge scale Limited queries Document MongoDB Flexible records, nested data Consistency across documents Wide-column Cassandra High write rates across regions Query design must come first Search Elasticsearch, OpenSearch Full text and filtering Not a primary source of truth Time series Prometheus, TimescaleDB Metrics over time High cardinality cost Consistency in plain terms When data is spread over several machines, a network partition forces a choice: keep answering ( availability ) and risk stale answers, or refuse some requests to stay correct ( consistency ). This is the idea behind CAP. Many stores offer eventual consistency : replicas converge shortly after a write, but a read right away may be stale. Some let you pick per request, for instance strongly consistent reads at higher cost. Caching A cache keeps copies of expensive results in fast memory. Common patterns: Cache-aside : the application checks the cache, on a miss reads the database and writes the result back with an expiry (TTL) Write-through : writes go to the cache and the database together Invalidate on change : delete or update the cached item when the source changes Failure modes to plan for: Cold cache : after a restart every request misses and hits the database at once Stampede : a popular key expires and thousands of requests rebuild it together. Add jitter to TTLs and let only one request rebuild. Stale data : choose TTLs deliberately and invalidate on writes Cache as the only copy : if you cannot rebuild it, it is a database and needs backups and persistence Operating Redis safely redis-cli INFO memory | grep -E \"used_memory_human|maxmemory_human\" redis-cli INFO stats | grep -E \"keyspace_hits|keyspace_misses|evicted_keys\" redis-cli --scan --pattern &#x27;session:*&#x27; | head redis-cli SLOWLOG GET 5 maxmemory 2gb maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru appendonly yes Set maxmemory and an eviction policy, or Redis will grow until the host runs out of memory Never run KEYS * on production, because it blocks the single-threaded server. Use SCAN . Persistence ( appendonly , snapshots) is optional for a pure cache and important for queues or sessions Do not expose Redis to the internet. Use a private network, authentication and TLS. How to choose Ask in order: What are the main queries and how often? Start from reads and writes. Do you need transactions and joins across entities? Pick relational. How much data and what write rate in a year? Does one node suffice? What consistency can users tolerate? Who will operate it, and do you have backups, monitoring and skills for it? Is a managed service available? A boring relational database plus a cache covers a very large share of real systems. Adding another store adds a new thing to back up, secure, monitor and patch. Predict first. A popular product page is cached for 60 seconds. At the moment it expires, 5,000 requests arrive. What can happen and how do you reduce it? Your guess Answer: All 5,000 miss at once and hit the database to rebuild the same page, a cache stampede. Use a lock so one request rebuilds, serve slightly stale data while refreshing, and add random jitter to TTLs. A cache protects the database only while it is warm. Cache-aside read path Flow in order: 1, Application to Cache; 2, Application to Database; 3, Database to Store with TTL; 4, Store with TTL to Cache. Service Cache Da"},"databases/postgres-vacuum-bloat-and-maintenance":{"html":"\u003cp>A table holds 2 million live rows and occupies 40 GB. Queries get slower every week. The cause is not the data; it is dead row versions that vacuum has not been able to clean. Postgres keeps old versions for concurrent readers, and maintenance decides whether that is harmless or costly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why updates and deletes leave dead rows\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What vacuum and autovacuum do\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Finding bloat and blockers such as long transactions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Routine maintenance and safe fixes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dead-rows-and-mvcc\">Dead rows and MVCC\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Postgres never edits a row in place. An update writes a new version and marks the old one as dead once no transaction can still see it. Dead versions occupy space until \u003cstrong>vacuum\u003c/strong> marks it reusable. If dead space grows faster than it is cleaned, tables and indexes \u003cstrong>bloat\u003c/strong>: they use more disk, more cache and more time per scan.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"autovacuum\">Autovacuum\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The autovacuum daemon vacuums and analyzes tables automatically when changes pass a threshold: by default 20 percent of the table plus 50 rows. On a huge, busy table, 20 percent is far too late, so tune it per table:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ALTER TABLE orders SET (\n  autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor = 0.02,\n  autovacuum_analyze_scale_factor = 0.01\n);\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ALTER TABLE\u003c/span> orders \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SET\u003c/span> (\n  autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.02\u003c/span>,\n  autovacuum_analyze_scale_factor \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.01\u003c/span>\n);\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The same daemon protects against \u003cstrong>transaction ID wraparound\u003c/strong>, a rare but severe condition in which the database stops accepting writes. Monitor the age of the oldest transaction ID and alert well before the limit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-blocks-vacuum\">What blocks vacuum\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Vacuum cannot remove a dead row that an old transaction might still see. Long-running transactions, forgotten sessions &quot;idle in transaction&quot;, abandoned replication slots and stuck prepared transactions hold back cleanup.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT pid, state, now() - xact_start AS age, left(query, 60) AS query\nFROM pg_stat_activity\nWHERE xact_start IS NOT NULL\nORDER BY xact_start\nLIMIT 5;\n\nSELECT relname, n_live_tup, n_dead_tup, last_autovacuum\nFROM pg_stat_user_tables\nORDER BY n_dead_tup DESC\nLIMIT 5;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> pid, state, now() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">-\u003c/span> xact_start \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> age, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">left\u003c/span>(query, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">60\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> query\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_activity\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> xact_start \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">IS\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> xact_start\nLIMIT \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>;\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> relname, n_live_tup, n_dead_tup, last_autovacuum\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_user_tables\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> n_dead_tup \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC\u003c/span>\nLIMIT \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Set \u003ccode>idle_in_transaction_session_timeout\u003c/code> so a forgotten session cannot block cleanup for days.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"routine-care-and-fixes\">Routine care and fixes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Plain \u003ccode>VACUUM\u003c/code> does not shrink the file; it makes space reusable. That is usually enough.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>VACUUM FULL\u003c/code> rewrites the table and takes an exclusive lock, so use it rarely, in a window. Tools such as \u003ccode>pg_repack\u003c/code> rebuild with only a short lock.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rebuild bloated indexes with \u003ccode>REINDEX INDEX CONCURRENTLY\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep statistics fresh with \u003ccode>ANALYZE\u003c/code>; the planner depends on them.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Watch dead tuple counts, table size growth, and the oldest transaction age as regular dashboard items.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Autovacuum runs often but dead rows keep growing and the table is bloated. What is a likely cause?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A long-running or idle-in-transaction session (or an old replication slot) holds back cleanup.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Vacuum can only remove rows that no one can still see.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-table-that-kept-growing\">Incident: The table that kept growing\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A queue-like table with constant updates grew from 5 GB to 60 GB in a month, and dashboards slowed down.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">=&gt; SELECT pid, state, now()-xact_start AS age FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state='idle in transaction';\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  pid  |        state         |      age\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 4172  | idle in transaction  | 11 days 03:12:44\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A reporting tool had left a transaction open for eleven days, so vacuum could not remove any row version created since. The session was ended, a timeout for idle transactions was added, and the table was rebuilt online with pg_repack. Per-table autovacuum settings and an alert on the oldest transaction age keep it from happening again.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using VACUUM FULL on a busy production table\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving autovacuum thresholds at the defaults for huge tables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring idle-in-transaction sessions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not monitoring transaction ID age\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"dead-rows-and-mvcc","text":"Dead rows and MVCC"},{"id":"autovacuum","text":"Autovacuum"},{"id":"what-blocks-vacuum","text":"What blocks vacuum"},{"id":"routine-care-and-fixes","text":"Routine care and fixes"},{"id":"incident-the-table-that-kept-growing","text":"Incident: The table that kept growing"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL: Routine vacuuming","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/routine-vacuuming.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL: Monitoring database activity","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/monitoring-stats.html"}],"text":"A table holds 2 million live rows and occupies 40 GB. Queries get slower every week. The cause is not the data; it is dead row versions that vacuum has not been able to clean. Postgres keeps old versions for concurrent readers, and maintenance decides whether that is harmless or costly. What you will learn Why updates and deletes leave dead rows What vacuum and autovacuum do Finding bloat and blockers such as long transactions Routine maintenance and safe fixes Dead rows and MVCC Postgres never edits a row in place. An update writes a new version and marks the old one as dead once no transaction can still see it. Dead versions occupy space until vacuum marks it reusable. If dead space grows faster than it is cleaned, tables and indexes bloat : they use more disk, more cache and more time per scan. Autovacuum The autovacuum daemon vacuums and analyzes tables automatically when changes pass a threshold: by default 20 percent of the table plus 50 rows. On a huge, busy table, 20 percent is far too late, so tune it per table: ALTER TABLE orders SET ( autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor = 0.02 , autovacuum_analyze_scale_factor = 0.01 ); The same daemon protects against transaction ID wraparound , a rare but severe condition in which the database stops accepting writes. Monitor the age of the oldest transaction ID and alert well before the limit. What blocks vacuum Vacuum cannot remove a dead row that an old transaction might still see. Long-running transactions, forgotten sessions \"idle in transaction\", abandoned replication slots and stuck prepared transactions hold back cleanup. SELECT pid, state, now() - xact_start AS age, left (query, 60 ) AS query FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE xact_start IS NOT NULL ORDER BY xact_start LIMIT 5 ; SELECT relname, n_live_tup, n_dead_tup, last_autovacuum FROM pg_stat_user_tables ORDER BY n_dead_tup DESC LIMIT 5 ; Set idle_in_transaction_session_timeout so a forgotten session cannot block cleanup for days. Routine care and fixes Plain VACUUM does not shrink the file; it makes space reusable. That is usually enough. VACUUM FULL rewrites the table and takes an exclusive lock, so use it rarely, in a window. Tools such as pg_repack rebuild with only a short lock. Rebuild bloated indexes with REINDEX INDEX CONCURRENTLY . Keep statistics fresh with ANALYZE ; the planner depends on them. Watch dead tuple counts, table size growth, and the oldest transaction age as regular dashboard items. Predict first. Autovacuum runs often but dead rows keep growing and the table is bloated. What is a likely cause? Your guess Answer: A long-running or idle-in-transaction session (or an old replication slot) holds back cleanup. Vacuum can only remove rows that no one can still see. Incident: The table that kept growing Incident A queue-like table with constant updates grew from 5 GB to 60 GB in a month, and dashboards slowed down. => SELECT pid, state, now()-xact_start AS age FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state='idle in transaction'; pid | state | age 4172 | idle in transaction | 11 days 03:12:44 A reporting tool had left a transaction open for eleven days, so vacuum could not remove any row version created since. The session was ended, a timeout for idle transactions was added, and the table was rebuilt online with pg_repack. Per-table autovacuum settings and an alert on the oldest transaction age keep it from happening again. Common mistakes Using VACUUM FULL on a busy production table Leaving autovacuum thresholds at the defaults for huge tables Ignoring idle-in-transaction sessions Not monitoring transaction ID age"},"databases/schema-design-for-operations":{"html":"\u003cp>The application team picked a text column for dates, no constraints, and one giant table for everything. It worked in the demo. A year later, bad data is everywhere, every migration locks the table, and nobody can say what a column means. Good design is partly an operations decision.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Keys and types that age well\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Constraints as a safety net\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Layouts that ease growth: partitioning and archiving\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Designing for change: nullable columns, defaults and naming\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keys-and-types\">Keys and types\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use a stable \u003cstrong>primary key\u003c/strong> that never changes and carries no business meaning: a bigint identity or a UUID. Use proper types: \u003ccode>timestamptz\u003c/code> for times (stored in UTC), \u003ccode>numeric\u003c/code> for money, \u003ccode>boolean\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>jsonb\u003c/code> for flexible parts. Dates stored as text cannot be compared, indexed or validated correctly. Pick sizes with growth in mind: an \u003ccode>integer\u003c/code> key overflows at about 2.1 billion rows, and changing it later means rewriting the table.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"constraints\">Constraints\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Let the database refuse bad data:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"CREATE TABLE payments (\n  id          bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  order_id    bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES orders(id),\n  amount      numeric(12,2) NOT NULL CHECK (amount &gt; 0),\n  currency    char(3) NOT NULL,\n  status      text NOT NULL CHECK (status IN ('pending','paid','failed')),\n  created_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n  UNIQUE (order_id, created_at)\n);\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE TABLE\u003c/span> payments (\n  id          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">bigint\u003c/span> GENERATED ALWAYS \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">IDENTITY\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">PRIMARY KEY\u003c/span>,\n  order_id    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">bigint\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">REFERENCES\u003c/span> orders(id),\n  amount      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">numeric\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">12\u003c/span>,\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CHECK\u003c/span> (amount \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>),\n  currency    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">char\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL\u003c/span>,\n  status      text \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CHECK\u003c/span> (status \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">IN\u003c/span> (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;pending&#x27;\u003c/span>,\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;paid&#x27;\u003c/span>,\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;failed&#x27;\u003c/span>)),\n  created_at  timestamptz \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DEFAULT\u003c/span> now(),\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">UNIQUE\u003c/span> (order_id, created_at)\n);\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>NOT NULL\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>CHECK\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>UNIQUE\u003c/code> and foreign keys are cheap and catch bugs from every application, script and manual fix. Add them from the start; adding them later requires cleaning existing data first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"layouts-for-growth\">Layouts for growth\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Tables that grow without limit (events, logs, payments) become slow to vacuum, back up and index. Use \u003cstrong>partitioning\u003c/strong> by time so old data sits in separate partitions that you can detach and archive quickly:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"CREATE TABLE events (\n  id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY,\n  happened_at timestamptz NOT NULL,\n  payload jsonb\n) PARTITION BY RANGE (happened_at);\n\nCREATE TABLE events_2026_01 PARTITION OF events\n  FOR VALUES FROM ('2026-01-01') TO ('2026-02-01');\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE TABLE\u003c/span> events (\n  id \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">bigint\u003c/span> GENERATED ALWAYS \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">IDENTITY\u003c/span>,\n  happened_at timestamptz \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">NOT NULL\u003c/span>,\n  payload jsonb\n) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">RANGE\u003c/span> (happened_at);\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CREATE TABLE\u003c/span> events_2026_01 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">OF\u003c/span> events\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FOR\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">VALUES\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2026-01-01&#x27;\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">TO\u003c/span> (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2026-02-01&#x27;\u003c/span>);\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Dropping a month is then an instant \u003ccode>DROP TABLE\u003c/code>, not a slow delete that leaves bloat. Automate partition creation so you never run out of them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"designing-for-change\">Designing for change\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Make future migrations safe. Add new columns as nullable or with a cheap default, backfill in batches, then add constraints. Avoid renaming columns used by running code without a compatibility period. Keep names clear and consistent (\u003ccode>created_at\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>customer_id\u003c/code>), and document the meaning of non-obvious columns with \u003ccode>COMMENT ON\u003c/code>. Keep rarely used wide data (big documents, blobs) out of hot tables. Review each schema change with a question: what happens when this table has a hundred times more rows?\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Why is storing dates as text a poor design choice?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The database cannot validate, compare, index or do arithmetic on them reliably, and bad values get in.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Types are constraints too.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-table-nobody-could-change\">Incident: The table nobody could change\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A 900 million row events table needed a new column, and the migration locked writes long enough to cause an outage.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">=&gt; ALTER TABLE events ALTER COLUMN user_id TYPE bigint;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-- waiting for ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock ...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">=&gt; SELECT count(*) FROM events;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  count\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-----------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 912330417\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team had used an integer key that was about to overflow, in a single unpartitioned table. The fix needed a phased plan: a new partitioned table, dual writes, batched copy, and a switch. Afterward the company adopted schema guidelines: bigint keys, timestamptz, constraints from the start, time partitioning for event tables, and a review checklist for every migration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Integer keys on tables that can grow large\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Missing constraints, so bad data accumulates\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One unbounded table with no archive plan\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Renaming or dropping columns without a compatibility period\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"keys-and-types","text":"Keys and types"},{"id":"constraints","text":"Constraints"},{"id":"layouts-for-growth","text":"Layouts for growth"},{"id":"designing-for-change","text":"Designing for change"},{"id":"incident-the-table-nobody-could-change","text":"Incident: The table nobody could change"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL: Table partitioning","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-partitioning.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL: Constraints","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-constraints.html"}],"text":"The application team picked a text column for dates, no constraints, and one giant table for everything. It worked in the demo. A year later, bad data is everywhere, every migration locks the table, and nobody can say what a column means. Good design is partly an operations decision. What you will learn Keys and types that age well Constraints as a safety net Layouts that ease growth: partitioning and archiving Designing for change: nullable columns, defaults and naming Keys and types Use a stable primary key that never changes and carries no business meaning: a bigint identity or a UUID. Use proper types: timestamptz for times (stored in UTC), numeric for money, boolean , jsonb for flexible parts. Dates stored as text cannot be compared, indexed or validated correctly. Pick sizes with growth in mind: an integer key overflows at about 2.1 billion rows, and changing it later means rewriting the table. Constraints Let the database refuse bad data: CREATE TABLE payments ( id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY , order_id bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES orders(id), amount numeric ( 12 , 2 ) NOT NULL CHECK (amount > 0 ), currency char ( 3 ) NOT NULL , status text NOT NULL CHECK (status IN ( &#x27;pending&#x27; , &#x27;paid&#x27; , &#x27;failed&#x27; )), created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(), UNIQUE (order_id, created_at) ); NOT NULL , CHECK , UNIQUE and foreign keys are cheap and catch bugs from every application, script and manual fix. Add them from the start; adding them later requires cleaning existing data first. Layouts for growth Tables that grow without limit (events, logs, payments) become slow to vacuum, back up and index. Use partitioning by time so old data sits in separate partitions that you can detach and archive quickly: CREATE TABLE events ( id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY , happened_at timestamptz NOT NULL , payload jsonb ) PARTITION BY RANGE (happened_at); CREATE TABLE events_2026_01 PARTITION OF events FOR VALUES FROM ( &#x27;2026-01-01&#x27; ) TO ( &#x27;2026-02-01&#x27; ); Dropping a month is then an instant DROP TABLE , not a slow delete that leaves bloat. Automate partition creation so you never run out of them. Designing for change Make future migrations safe. Add new columns as nullable or with a cheap default, backfill in batches, then add constraints. Avoid renaming columns used by running code without a compatibility period. Keep names clear and consistent ( created_at , customer_id ), and document the meaning of non-obvious columns with COMMENT ON . Keep rarely used wide data (big documents, blobs) out of hot tables. Review each schema change with a question: what happens when this table has a hundred times more rows? Predict first. Why is storing dates as text a poor design choice? Your guess Answer: The database cannot validate, compare, index or do arithmetic on them reliably, and bad values get in. Types are constraints too. Incident: The table nobody could change Incident A 900 million row events table needed a new column, and the migration locked writes long enough to cause an outage. => ALTER TABLE events ALTER COLUMN user_id TYPE bigint; -- waiting for ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock ... => SELECT count(*) FROM events; count ----------- 912330417 The team had used an integer key that was about to overflow, in a single unpartitioned table. The fix needed a phased plan: a new partitioned table, dual writes, batched copy, and a switch. Afterward the company adopted schema guidelines: bigint keys, timestamptz, constraints from the start, time partitioning for event tables, and a review checklist for every migration. Common mistakes Integer keys on tables that can grow large Missing constraints, so bad data accumulates One unbounded table with no archive plan Renaming or dropping columns without a compatibility period"},"databases/debugging-database-problems":{"html":"\u003cp>Database incidents are tense because the data matters and everything depends on it. A fixed order of checks keeps you calm and stops you from making an unrecoverable change in a hurry.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A triage order for the four common symptoms\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Queries for connections, locks, disk and replication\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Safe emergency actions and their costs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>When to stop and protect the data\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"principle-do-no-harm-first\">Principle: do no harm first\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Before you kill, restart, failover or delete anything, ask: can this lose data, and is there a recent backup? Look before you act, take notes of what you ran, and prefer reversible steps (cancel a query before killing a connection, add capacity before restarting).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"symptom-1-it-is-slow\">Symptom 1: it is slow\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Host: CPU, memory, disk latency (\u003ccode>iostat -x 1\u003c/code>), swap\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sessions: how many active, how many waiting\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT wait_event_type, wait_event, count(*)\nFROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state = 'active'\nGROUP BY 1, 2 ORDER BY 3 DESC;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> wait_event_type, wait_event, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">count\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">*\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_activity \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> state \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;active&#x27;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003col start=\"3\">\n\u003cli>Top queries in the last interval (\u003ccode>pg_stat_statements\u003c/code>), and the plan of the worst\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Recent changes: deploy, migration, traffic spike, stale statistics (\u003ccode>ANALYZE\u003c/code>)\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2 id=\"symptom-2-queries-hang\">Symptom 2: queries hang\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Look for blocking: long transactions, a queued \u003ccode>ALTER\u003c/code>, an \u003ccode>idle in transaction\u003c/code> session.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT pid, pg_blocking_pids(pid) AS blocked_by, state, left(query, 60)\nFROM pg_stat_activity WHERE cardinality(pg_blocking_pids(pid)) &gt; 0;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> pid, pg_blocking_pids(pid) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> blocked_by, state, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">left\u003c/span>(query, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">60\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_activity \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">cardinality\u003c/span>(pg_blocking_pids(pid)) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Cancel the \u003cstrong>root\u003c/strong> blocker, not the victims: \u003ccode>SELECT pg_cancel_backend(PID);\u003c/code> first, then \u003ccode>pg_terminate_backend(PID)\u003c/code> if needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"symptom-3-out-of-space\">Symptom 3: out of space\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"df -h /var/lib/postgresql\ndu -sh /var/lib/postgresql/16/main/* | sort -h | tail -5\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -h /var/lib/postgresql\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">du\u003c/span> -sh /var/lib/postgresql/16/main/* | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> -h | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -5\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT slot_name, active, pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), restart_lsn)) AS retained\nFROM pg_replication_slots;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> slot_name, active, pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), restart_lsn)) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> retained\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_replication_slots;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Common causes: write-ahead logs piling up because of a stuck replica or an unused replication slot, a failing archive command, runaway temp files, bloat, or a huge table that grew. Free space safely by fixing the cause (drop an abandoned slot, repair archiving), or adding disk. \u003cstrong>Never delete files inside the data directory by hand\u003c/strong>, because that can destroy the database.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"symptom-4-cannot-connect\">Symptom 4: cannot connect\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pg_isready -h db1.example.com -p 5432\nss -tlnp | grep 5432\njournalctl -u postgresql --since &quot;10 min ago&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">pg_isready -h db1.example.com -p 5432\nss -tlnp | grep 5432\njournalctl -u postgresql --since \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10 min ago&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Check in order: is the process up, is it listening, can the network reach it (firewall, security group, DNS), is it out of connections (\u003ccode>FATAL: too many clients\u003c/code>), does authentication fail (\u003ccode>pg_hba.conf\u003c/code>, password, TLS), did a failover move the primary?\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"replication-problems\">Replication problems\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SELECT client_addr, state, pg_wal_lsn_diff(sent_lsn, replay_lsn) AS bytes_behind\nFROM pg_stat_replication;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT\u003c/span> client_addr, state, pg_wal_lsn_diff(sent_lsn, replay_lsn) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS\u003c/span> bytes_behind\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> pg_stat_replication;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A lagging replica may need a long query cancelled, more IO, or a rebuild from a fresh base backup.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"corruption-and-recovery\">Corruption and recovery\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If you see checksum errors, missing files or crash loops, stop writing, \u003cstrong>copy the data directory aside if you can\u003c/strong>, and use your restore procedure rather than experimenting on the only copy. Document the timeline for the review.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> The data disk is 98 percent full and growing, and an unused replication slot shows 400 GB retained. What do you do?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Confirm the slot is truly abandoned, then drop it with pg_drop_replication_slot so the retained logs can be recycled. Do not delete WAL files by hand.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The database knows what it still needs. The files do not tell you.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"sym&gt;look;look&gt;safe;safe&gt;prove\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Database incident triage\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sym\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Name the symptom\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"look\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Look without changing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"safe\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Safest action first\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prove\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Confirm recovery\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Name the symptom to Look without changing; 2, Look without changing to Safest action first; 3, Safest action first to Confirm recovery.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sym\">\u003cdt>Name the symptom \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Slow, hanging, full or unreachable.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Mixing symptoms sends you down the wrong path.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"look\">\u003cdt>Look without changing \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sessions, locks, disk, replication, logs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Acting before looking can lose data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"safe\">\u003cdt>Safest action first \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Cancel a query, drop a stale slot, add capacity.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Restarts hide the evidence and drop the cache.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prove\">\u003cdt>Confirm recovery \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Latency, errors and replication back to normal.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Declaring it fixed too early invites a repeat.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-full-disk-from-a-forgotten-slot\">Incident: the full disk from a forgotten slot\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A replica was decommissioned but its replication slot stayed. The primary kept every write-ahead log segment for it, filled the disk in the early hours and stopped accepting writes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;SELECT slot_name, active FROM pg_replication_slots&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT slot_name, active FROM pg_replication_slots&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> slot_name | active\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-----------+--------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> replica_old | f\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team dropped the slot, the logs were recycled and writes resumed. They added alerts on disk free space, slot lag and WAL size, and a checklist item to remove slots when removing replicas.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Killing victim sessions instead of the root blocker\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting files in the data directory\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restarting before capturing evidence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running heavy diagnostic queries on an overloaded primary without limits\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"principle-do-no-harm-first","text":"Principle: do no harm first"},{"id":"symptom-1-it-is-slow","text":"Symptom 1: it is slow"},{"id":"symptom-2-queries-hang","text":"Symptom 2: queries hang"},{"id":"symptom-3-out-of-space","text":"Symptom 3: out of space"},{"id":"symptom-4-cannot-connect","text":"Symptom 4: cannot connect"},{"id":"replication-problems","text":"Replication problems"},{"id":"corruption-and-recovery","text":"Corruption and recovery"},{"id":"incident-the-full-disk-from-a-forgotten-slot","text":"Incident: the full disk from a forgotten slot"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Monitoring database activity","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/monitoring-stats.html"},{"t":"Explicit locking","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/explicit-locking.html"},{"t":"Replication slots","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/warm-standby.html"}],"text":"Database incidents are tense because the data matters and everything depends on it. A fixed order of checks keeps you calm and stops you from making an unrecoverable change in a hurry. What you will learn A triage order for the four common symptoms Queries for connections, locks, disk and replication Safe emergency actions and their costs When to stop and protect the data Principle: do no harm first Before you kill, restart, failover or delete anything, ask: can this lose data, and is there a recent backup? Look before you act, take notes of what you ran, and prefer reversible steps (cancel a query before killing a connection, add capacity before restarting). Symptom 1: it is slow Host: CPU, memory, disk latency ( iostat -x 1 ), swap Sessions: how many active, how many waiting SELECT wait_event_type, wait_event, count ( * ) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state = &#x27;active&#x27; GROUP BY 1 , 2 ORDER BY 3 DESC ; Top queries in the last interval ( pg_stat_statements ), and the plan of the worst Recent changes: deploy, migration, traffic spike, stale statistics ( ANALYZE ) Symptom 2: queries hang Look for blocking: long transactions, a queued ALTER , an idle in transaction session. SELECT pid, pg_blocking_pids(pid) AS blocked_by, state, left (query, 60 ) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE cardinality (pg_blocking_pids(pid)) > 0 ; Cancel the root blocker, not the victims: SELECT pg_cancel_backend(PID); first, then pg_terminate_backend(PID) if needed. Symptom 3: out of space df -h /var/lib/postgresql du -sh /var/lib/postgresql/16/main/* | sort -h | tail -5 SELECT slot_name, active, pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), restart_lsn)) AS retained FROM pg_replication_slots; Common causes: write-ahead logs piling up because of a stuck replica or an unused replication slot, a failing archive command, runaway temp files, bloat, or a huge table that grew. Free space safely by fixing the cause (drop an abandoned slot, repair archiving), or adding disk. Never delete files inside the data directory by hand , because that can destroy the database. Symptom 4: cannot connect pg_isready -h db1.example.com -p 5432 ss -tlnp | grep 5432 journalctl -u postgresql --since \"10 min ago\" Check in order: is the process up, is it listening, can the network reach it (firewall, security group, DNS), is it out of connections ( FATAL: too many clients ), does authentication fail ( pg_hba.conf , password, TLS), did a failover move the primary? Replication problems SELECT client_addr, state, pg_wal_lsn_diff(sent_lsn, replay_lsn) AS bytes_behind FROM pg_stat_replication; A lagging replica may need a long query cancelled, more IO, or a rebuild from a fresh base backup. Corruption and recovery If you see checksum errors, missing files or crash loops, stop writing, copy the data directory aside if you can , and use your restore procedure rather than experimenting on the only copy. Document the timeline for the review. Predict first. The data disk is 98 percent full and growing, and an unused replication slot shows 400 GB retained. What do you do? Your guess Answer: Confirm the slot is truly abandoned, then drop it with pg_drop_replication_slot so the retained logs can be recycled. Do not delete WAL files by hand. The database knows what it still needs. The files do not tell you. Database incident triage Flow in order: 1, Name the symptom to Look without changing; 2, Look without changing to Safest action first; 3, Safest action first to Confirm recovery. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Name the symptom Step What it does: Slow, hanging, full or unreachable. If it fails or is weak: Mixing symptoms sends you down the wrong path. Look without changing Step What it does: Sessions, locks, disk, replication, logs. If it fails or is weak: Acting before looking can lose data. Safest action first Step What it does: Cancel a query, drop a stale slot, add capacity. If it fails or is we"}});